WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:06.330
- Good morning, everybody.

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:09.450
My name is Penny Dash and
I'm chair of NHS England.

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:11.880
And I welcome everyone who's viewing

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:14.490
and everyone in the room
to the board meeting

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:15.903
of NHS England.

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:19.470
We will start by doing a
quick round of introductions

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:22.680
of who's here so that
everyone who's either watching

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:25.260
or in the room knows who everyone is.

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:26.610
I'll start with Rob.

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:27.443
- Good morning.

00:00:27.443 --> 00:00:28.573
I'm Rob Checketts.

00:00:28.573 --> 00:00:29.589
I'm the exec director

00:00:29.589 --> 00:00:31.290
for Corporate Affairs and Communications.

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:32.520
- Morning, everybody.

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:33.660
My name's Sarah-Jane Marsh.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:36.270
I'm the NHS England
chief operating officer.

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:38.640
- David Bennett, non-executive director.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.650
- Glen Burley, deputy Chief Executive.

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:43.020
- Danny Mortimer, director
General for People.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.548
- Ravi Gurumurthy, the non-exec director.

00:00:45.548 --> 00:00:47.580
- Morning, Paul Dinkin, director general

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.620
for Strategy and Healthcare Policy.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:51.090
- Morning, Elizabeth O'Mahony,

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:53.700
DG Finance, CFO for NHS England.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:55.770
- Director general, I'm
not using an acronym.

00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:57.780
- Oh, director general, Finance.

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:00.630
- Morning, Jim Mackey,
chief exec NHS England.

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:01.710
- Penny Dash.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:04.410
- Mark Bailie, non-executive director.

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:07.680
- Rob Thompson, chief digital
data and technology officer.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.050
- Frankie Swords,
national medical director.

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:12.270
- Paul Corrigan, non-exec director.

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:13.650
- Good morning, Meghana Pandit,

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:15.647
deputy chief executive, NHS England.

00:01:15.647 --> 00:01:16.890
- Good morning, Duncan Burton,

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:18.930
chief nursing officer for England.

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:19.763
- Great.

00:01:19.763 --> 00:01:20.610
And Sam Everington, who's one

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:21.840
of the non-executive directors

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:24.123
will be joining us in about half an hour.

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:28.770
In particular, I'd like to welcome Frankie

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:31.053
and Rob to your first board meeting.

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:35.970
Apologies for absence
received from Louise Ansari,

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:38.250
Andrew Morris, Jeremy
Townsend, Robert Lechler,

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:39.843
and Fiona McBride.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:43.860
Are there any declarations
of interest from anyone

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.610
that haven't already been declared?

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:49.350
Okay, thank you.

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:52.380
And I'll now come onto the minutes

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:54.120
from the previous board meeting.

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.793
Does anyone have any comments on those?

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:00.863
No

00:02:00.863 --> 00:02:01.799
Okay.

00:02:01.799 --> 00:02:05.490
So we will take those
as approved and read.

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:06.570
Thank you.

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:08.550
So we're going to go first of all,

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:12.300
to hearing from the various
board subcommittees.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:16.650
We've had lots of meetings of
those and then we will come on

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:17.820
and go to risk management

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:21.150
and then to the chief execs update.

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:24.300
So who's going to do
people instead of Louise?

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:25.410
Okay, thank you, Sarah-Jane.

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:26.790
- Yeah, no, apologies from Louise.

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:28.350
She's unable to make
it at the last moment.

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:30.150
So there's two People Committees

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:33.420
that have taken place
since the last board,

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:36.180
the meeting on the 13th of April.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:38.970
There is a summary in the board pack

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:41.280
and then we had a meeting
on the 2nd of June.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.230
So I'll just update on that verbally.

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:44.790
Given there's quite a lot of crossover,

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:46.620
I think there's just one
thing that I would like

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:47.910
to highlight from the pack,

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:51.000
which is the NHS England
staff survey results,

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:53.160
which the committee spent
quite considerable time

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:55.440
talking about, was obviously concerned

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.050
about some of those results.

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:00.060
I think on reflecting on them,

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:02.580
obviously, some aspects are impacted

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:04.290
by the transformation
programme, which I'll come on

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:05.970
to talk about a little in a minute

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:08.160
and the degree of uncertainty

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:10.410
that many of our teams are going through.

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:12.630
But we also noted that
there were things in there

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:15.570
that have been recurrent themes
in NHS England staff surveys

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:17.520
that predate that as well.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:20.490
So the committee's really
keen that we take the approach

00:03:20.490 --> 00:03:23.160
that I know many NHS organisations take

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:26.460
is that we absolutely
focus on some key areas,

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:29.760
particularly those that
relate to staff engagement

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:31.440
and have a real focus on those

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:32.820
through the course of this year.

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:35.970
And then track improvement
via both the pulse surveys

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:38.490
that we are launching so we
can have more regular check-ins

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:40.290
with our teams

00:03:40.290 --> 00:03:42.990
and then a bigger survey
12 months on from this one,

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:44.880
which we will undertake.

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:47.190
So I don't think I'll
pull anything else out

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:48.090
from that set, Penny.

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:52.650
If I go then to the meeting
that we had this week,

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:54.690
there was an awful lot
of things to discuss.

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:57.720
I think the one, again,
the ones I'd pull out are,

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:00.480
we're just coming to
the end of the big cycle

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:04.243
of voluntary redundancy, which
colleagues will be aware of.

00:04:04.243 --> 00:04:06.600
I think it's obviously
been a massive strain

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:08.640
for the organisation as
a whole, both for some

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:10.050
of those colleagues that are leaving.

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:13.680
Although we have put a significant
support package in place,

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:15.420
many people have been here a long time.

00:04:15.420 --> 00:04:17.370
So you know, as teams have separated,

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:20.880
it's been a challenging
process for colleagues.

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:22.290
It's also because of the way

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:25.740
that the people leaving a distributed,

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:30.740
some teams are now significantly
changing in size and shape.

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:33.180
And I think that just the
technicalities of the process

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:34.470
that the HR team have led

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:36.150
through has been really
significant as well.

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:38.790
So we spent quite a lot
of time reflecting on that

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:41.640
and also lessons learned for any future,

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:45.300
voluntary redundancies that
we may need to undertake

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:47.190
through the course of the year.

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:50.970
We also talked about progress
on the regional consultation,

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:54.690
which the consultation
process itself has now closed

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:58.350
and during June, we will be
producing the outcome reports

00:04:58.350 --> 00:05:01.230
from that both on a region by region basis

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:04.200
where colleagues have
made specific comments

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:06.570
that are pertinent to
that regional structure

00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:09.240
but also more generally where
themes have come through

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:10.320
and the final structures

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:15.180
and filling of post-process
will be complete.

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:18.360
I just want to say a big
thank you for the way

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:20.040
that people have really engaged in this.

00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:22.230
We've had hundreds of staff
and they've really come at it

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:23.100
from the perspective

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:26.100
of what is going to make the
region best acting service

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:26.933
of the NHS.

00:05:26.933 --> 00:05:29.370
So there's a real lot of
thought going into that

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:30.872
and I think the people

00:05:30.872 --> 00:05:32.040
that are leading this process regionally

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:33.030
are really listening

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:37.920
and we will see some quite
significant changes to those

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:40.110
that the consultation
document when we come

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:42.510
to the final versions in due course.

00:05:42.510 --> 00:05:45.270
But again, it'll be a
recurrent theme on quite a few

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:47.910
of my comments that he's taken a lot

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:50.580
of regional bandwidth at present
when there's an awful lot

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:51.990
of other things going on as well

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:55.680
that we would want regional
teams to be focusing on.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:57.960
On the other, I suppose the things

00:05:57.960 --> 00:05:59.610
that we're trying to do then around it,

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:02.670
we talked about the organisational
development priorities

00:06:02.670 --> 00:06:04.590
and in particular, the importance

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:07.260
of really aligning the work

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:09.960
that people who remain in
the organisation are doing

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:11.430
with the things that impact

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:12.900
and make a difference in the NHS.

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:15.540
We know that means that
people feel more energised,

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:17.310
more engaged, they come out better

00:06:17.310 --> 00:06:18.990
in the staff survey results.

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:22.260
So how we align teams
around those priorities,

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:25.110
but also how we support
people on an individual level

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:27.630
with an improved learning
and development offer.

00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:30.870
We've just completely
overhauled the appraisal system

00:06:30.870 --> 00:06:32.010
and the way that appraisals

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:34.110
and things such things are recorded,

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:37.440
but also our sort of ongoing
wellbeing offers as well

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:39.930
and we've made sure that we prioritise

00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:43.298
those kind of important
activities in our non-pay budgets

00:06:43.298 --> 00:06:44.790
for this year.

00:06:44.790 --> 00:06:48.090
So I think in some reason, there is a lot

00:06:48.090 --> 00:06:50.580
of concern I think for the
people committee about the things

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:52.860
that our people are need,
where they currently are

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:53.910
and what they're going to need to go

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:56.370
through in subsequent months as well.

00:06:56.370 --> 00:06:58.830
I think there's assurance
that we are doing what we can

00:06:58.830 --> 00:07:02.520
as NHS England to mitigate
some of those risks

00:07:02.520 --> 00:07:05.520
but there will be more
uncertainty to follow

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:07.380
and I think Louise has challenged myself

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:08.940
and Lauren Hughes when working with

00:07:08.940 --> 00:07:11.520
on the improving NHS
England Together programme

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:14.010
that she wants to see more outcomes

00:07:14.010 --> 00:07:17.460
from some of the processes
that we've described as we go

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:18.750
through the meetings in the year.

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:20.340
So what does it really look like

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:22.683
to prioritise more ruthlessly and so on.

00:07:23.730 --> 00:07:26.760
So yes, continuing to feel
challenged by the committee

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:27.990
but I think rightly so.

00:07:27.990 --> 00:07:28.950
Thanks, Chair.

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:29.783
- Okay, thank you.

00:07:29.783 --> 00:07:32.820
And do you want to talk about
the transformation programme

00:07:32.820 --> 00:07:35.370
now, or should we pick that
up in the ARAC conversation?

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:37.980
- It's entirely up to you, Penny.

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:39.752
It probably fits with the ARAC-

00:07:39.752 --> 00:07:40.585
- I think we'll-

00:07:40.585 --> 00:07:41.418
- Because we'll try to come at it

00:07:41.418 --> 00:07:44.370
through a risk lens
yesterday rather than a sort

00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:46.086
of organisational development lens.

00:07:46.086 --> 00:07:47.137
- Okay, great.

00:07:47.137 --> 00:07:50.493
Do I have any questions, comments?

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:54.050
Okay, thank you for
doing that, Sarah-Jane.

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:59.610
So I will now talk about
the National Quality Board

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:02.013
and the NHS England Quality Committee,

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:05.160
I should say thank you
in particular for Meghana

00:08:05.160 --> 00:08:08.220
and Duncan who have helped
considerably on both of those

00:08:08.220 --> 00:08:11.940
and Frankie who will now be
picking up some of that as well.

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:13.650
So we have now moved

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:17.520
to a system whereby we have
the National Quality Board,

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:19.050
which includes a wider number

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:22.500
of organisations all
focused on quality of care

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:27.500
and then we follow that by the
NHS England Quality Committee

00:08:27.510 --> 00:08:29.850
to make sure that we can
be as aligned as possible

00:08:29.850 --> 00:08:32.970
but equally recognising
the statutory obligations

00:08:32.970 --> 00:08:35.490
of the NHS England Quality Committee.

00:08:35.490 --> 00:08:37.110
So we've been spending
a bit of time agreeing

00:08:37.110 --> 00:08:39.270
the relative terms of
reference for both of those

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:42.660
and I think we've got to a good place.

00:08:42.660 --> 00:08:46.020
We had a very inspiring
conversation looking

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:47.940
at a core metric set.

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:49.470
I think this is really impressive.

00:08:49.470 --> 00:08:51.420
I'd almost maybe go as far to say,

00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:54.090
I think other countries
internationally would be interested

00:08:54.090 --> 00:08:56.400
in looking at how we're
putting that together.

00:08:56.400 --> 00:08:58.800
It will give us a very
large number of metrics

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:01.410
but we will also have
the ability to drill up

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:02.520
and drill down in those

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:04.770
so that we can look at
things at different levels

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:08.430
and hopefully will give us a good overview

00:09:08.430 --> 00:09:10.740
of what's happening across quality

00:09:10.740 --> 00:09:12.645
of care in multiple areas,

00:09:12.645 --> 00:09:16.530
multiple domains of quality
across the whole country.

00:09:16.530 --> 00:09:20.320
And we as non-execs, we're very much

00:09:21.210 --> 00:09:23.610
seeking assurance from our
executive colleagues about,

00:09:23.610 --> 00:09:24.900
it's not just looking at the data,

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:27.690
it's then acting on the
data and how do we do that

00:09:27.690 --> 00:09:28.800
and how do we intervene

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:31.680
and how do we take
action when we see things

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:34.110
that aren't going in the right direction.

00:09:34.110 --> 00:09:35.670
And in particular as part of that,

00:09:35.670 --> 00:09:38.550
we are absolutely looking
at unwarranted variation

00:09:38.550 --> 00:09:40.980
and outliers but we are also
looking at the big picture

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:42.990
of what's happening in
terms of life expectancy

00:09:42.990 --> 00:09:44.370
of quality of life.

00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:46.710
And we'll be coming
back to that as we work

00:09:46.710 --> 00:09:48.780
through future strategic direction,

00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:52.860
picking up on the 10 year
plan as we move forward.

00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:54.453
So that's a key piece of work.

00:09:55.320 --> 00:09:57.390
We've been looking at
a few specific items,

00:09:57.390 --> 00:09:59.950
particularly around off-label medicines

00:10:01.050 --> 00:10:04.650
and as ever, looking
at how we look at risks

00:10:04.650 --> 00:10:05.757
as I just picked up,.

00:10:06.630 --> 00:10:10.950
We then took a paper on
Safe and Effective Staffing

00:10:10.950 --> 00:10:15.630
and this requires more work
which Duncan is leading on,

00:10:15.630 --> 00:10:18.180
but really looking at how
do we get the balance right

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:22.320
between sort of quite top-down
quite prescriptive models

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:24.960
versus allowing local organisations

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:27.030
to look at local services

00:10:27.030 --> 00:10:29.910
and how they can best adjust staffing

00:10:29.910 --> 00:10:32.433
to the local public and patient need.

00:10:33.660 --> 00:10:35.850
We have been spending a lot of time

00:10:35.850 --> 00:10:37.590
on the modern service frameworks

00:10:37.590 --> 00:10:41.010
and really pleased a number
of those are getting close

00:10:41.010 --> 00:10:44.400
to being ready for publication.

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:46.230
We'll be talking a bit more about those

00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:47.700
in the private board meeting.

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:50.490
But suffice to say that
they're all setting

00:10:50.490 --> 00:10:53.460
a very high level of ambition
for how do we improve quality

00:10:53.460 --> 00:10:56.430
of care across a number of
areas that they're looking

00:10:56.430 --> 00:10:58.500
at what the evidence base
tells us they're looking

00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:01.290
at where there are gaps
in current service models,

00:11:01.290 --> 00:11:03.540
they're looking at future delivery models,

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:05.700
they're looking at the way
in which we'll use technology

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:07.200
to underpin those

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:08.910
and they're looking at what it will take

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:12.510
to deliver against those
in a meaningful timeframe.

00:11:12.510 --> 00:11:16.020
So all of that as I say,
feeling very positive,

00:11:16.020 --> 00:11:19.803
so I'll stop at that point
on the quality agendas.

00:11:20.760 --> 00:11:23.880
Any questions, comments?

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:25.740
And I'm having to work
half of my money today

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:26.910
'cause I can't go on and talk

00:11:26.910 --> 00:11:31.910
about the research
innovation and growth board.

00:11:32.700 --> 00:11:35.250
So this is a new subcommittee of the board

00:11:35.250 --> 00:11:37.540
that we have set up very explicitly works

00:11:38.480 --> 00:11:40.950
across NHS England and DHSC

00:11:40.950 --> 00:11:43.045
and is also working very closely

00:11:43.045 --> 00:11:47.070
with the life sciences
sector plan, which is more

00:11:47.070 --> 00:11:50.100
of a pan government agenda focused on

00:11:50.100 --> 00:11:53.220
as well as improving
access to life sciences

00:11:53.220 --> 00:11:54.330
that also supports

00:11:54.330 --> 00:11:59.330
and reinforces our wider
growth agenda as a country.

00:11:59.550 --> 00:12:01.203
We had a very interesting presentation

00:12:01.203 --> 00:12:06.203
from the AI Commission which is looking

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:08.850
at how do we regulate new technologies,

00:12:08.850 --> 00:12:11.370
what are new technologies
that are coming on stream,

00:12:11.370 --> 00:12:14.790
how do we interface that
with again, wider government.

00:12:14.790 --> 00:12:17.190
So all of that very interesting
with a particular focus

00:12:17.190 --> 00:12:20.760
for us in the here and now
on ambient voice technology,

00:12:20.760 --> 00:12:23.730
also known as AVT.

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:26.070
And that's something we're
going to need coming back

00:12:26.070 --> 00:12:29.283
to how do we make sure that we
appropriately regulate that.

00:12:30.150 --> 00:12:34.920
We've looked at how we think
about health technologies,

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:36.540
medical technologies,

00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:39.660
how those are introduced
in a way into the service

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:42.300
which support and encourage
adoption of the best ones

00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:44.700
but also how we take
out the ineffective ones

00:12:44.700 --> 00:12:46.110
of which there are some.

00:12:46.110 --> 00:12:48.660
And that thinking about how we embed that

00:12:48.660 --> 00:12:50.490
very much into the modern
service frameworks.

00:12:50.490 --> 00:12:52.260
So that was a very good conversation.

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:55.080
And then we heard about the work

00:12:55.080 --> 00:12:58.350
of the Health Innovation
Networks, the HINs,

00:12:58.350 --> 00:13:00.690
that are really bodies that
are there on the ground

00:13:00.690 --> 00:13:03.150
to take innovation and to deliver it.

00:13:03.150 --> 00:13:05.822
I think suffice to say
we do see some variation

00:13:05.822 --> 00:13:07.320
in their work and that's
something that we want

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:09.513
to be looking at in a bit more detail.

00:13:11.550 --> 00:13:13.050
Any comments, questions?

00:13:13.050 --> 00:13:13.883
Okay, good.

00:13:13.883 --> 00:13:16.710
So I can hand back it over to others.

00:13:16.710 --> 00:13:20.820
So next one up is the Data
Digital and Technology Committee.

00:13:20.820 --> 00:13:21.702
Mark.

00:13:21.702 --> 00:13:22.560
- Thank you, Penny.

00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:26.670
So the Data Digital and
Technology Committee

00:13:26.670 --> 00:13:28.410
and the Cyber Risk Committee took place

00:13:28.410 --> 00:13:30.663
on the 1st and 2nd of June respectively.

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:34.320
Starting with cyber and resilience.

00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:37.590
I think there's really good progress

00:13:37.590 --> 00:13:39.330
on the resilience programme.

00:13:39.330 --> 00:13:41.310
It's a big estate, the
team have been working

00:13:41.310 --> 00:13:45.870
to it diligently and I think
we were particularly pleased

00:13:45.870 --> 00:13:50.220
with the willingness to
adopt new ways of working

00:13:50.220 --> 00:13:53.520
and openness to external challenge,

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:55.560
which clearly brings more information

00:13:55.560 --> 00:13:56.500
that you then have to react to.

00:13:56.500 --> 00:13:59.583
But I think the openness
of the teams was fantastic.

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:04.470
The reality of large
legacy technology estates

00:14:04.470 --> 00:14:08.070
is we're inevitably
exposed to big cyber risk.

00:14:08.070 --> 00:14:10.740
That cyber risk is persistent
and I think in our world,

00:14:10.740 --> 00:14:14.010
given its direct effect on
operational performance,

00:14:14.010 --> 00:14:16.770
this is a direct patient safety issue

00:14:16.770 --> 00:14:21.180
and so remains top of the priorities.

00:14:21.180 --> 00:14:23.163
We also discussed at length, look,

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:27.690
without getting too
technical, there are a series

00:14:27.690 --> 00:14:31.950
of new large language models like Mythos

00:14:31.950 --> 00:14:34.530
from Anthropic which will be released

00:14:34.530 --> 00:14:36.900
in the next four to six weeks.

00:14:36.900 --> 00:14:41.100
These undoubtedly deliver
a huge amount of capability

00:14:41.100 --> 00:14:44.310
for good and bad about
detecting vulnerabilities

00:14:44.310 --> 00:14:46.378
and how you might manage it

00:14:46.378 --> 00:14:51.300
and therefore, the attack
surface will materially increase

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:52.860
over the coming weeks.

00:14:52.860 --> 00:14:55.440
And I think the executive
have lent into this

00:14:55.440 --> 00:14:59.970
about how we reprioritize,
refocus to make sure

00:14:59.970 --> 00:15:03.423
we are as well placed as
possible for that reality.

00:15:06.060 --> 00:15:07.740
The key to this is as the
risk change you've got

00:15:07.740 --> 00:15:10.080
to change the approach and
the team are taking it on.

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:12.330
And I think with full support from Jim,

00:15:12.330 --> 00:15:13.380
which has been great.

00:15:14.670 --> 00:15:16.980
In terms of transformation,

00:15:16.980 --> 00:15:19.950
we concentrated a lot on voice to text.

00:15:19.950 --> 00:15:24.663
So in the attempt of
missing acronyms, so AVT,

00:15:26.070 --> 00:15:28.570
the federated data platform, which gives

00:15:30.150 --> 00:15:33.360
our key operational leaders
the ability to get to data

00:15:33.360 --> 00:15:35.403
and manage the acute system much better.

00:15:36.300 --> 00:15:39.753
Single patient record and Wayfinder.

00:15:39.753 --> 00:15:43.110
And Wayfinder is the bit of
technology that allows you

00:15:43.110 --> 00:15:46.650
to manage a, well, both clinicians

00:15:46.650 --> 00:15:48.690
and patients to manage their relationship

00:15:48.690 --> 00:15:49.890
with the acute system.

00:15:49.890 --> 00:15:52.140
So whether it's appointment booking

00:15:52.140 --> 00:15:54.873
or pre-consultation questionnaires.

00:15:56.100 --> 00:16:00.540
All of them have huge benefits

00:16:00.540 --> 00:16:03.810
in terms of both experience
and productivity for patients,

00:16:03.810 --> 00:16:07.500
colleagues and the system more widely.

00:16:07.500 --> 00:16:11.400
I think what struck us most
though were there are a series

00:16:11.400 --> 00:16:16.400
of cross-cutting issues
that affect all of them,

00:16:18.540 --> 00:16:21.000
making sure there's efficient,
capable resource at the point

00:16:21.000 --> 00:16:24.630
of delivery, clear business
ownership of change,

00:16:24.630 --> 00:16:26.940
robust measurement and
reporting of where we are

00:16:26.940 --> 00:16:30.870
and the speed of progress,

00:16:30.870 --> 00:16:34.200
agreement on how you do
prioritisation and trade-offs,

00:16:34.200 --> 00:16:36.660
agreement on how you realise benefits

00:16:36.660 --> 00:16:39.180
and clear accountability for outcomes,

00:16:39.180 --> 00:16:42.300
which are all need improvement.

00:16:42.300 --> 00:16:45.117
Now, we are getting to the changes

00:16:45.117 --> 00:16:47.520
and any large organisation
gets to this point

00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:50.070
where the cross-cutting
stuff is the difficult things

00:16:50.070 --> 00:16:53.190
and the fact that the executive
are now leaning into this

00:16:53.190 --> 00:16:58.190
and working out how we
manage those trade offs.

00:16:58.800 --> 00:17:01.020
And I think between Rob and Paul,

00:17:01.020 --> 00:17:03.090
we'll be seeing something
at the annual strategy day

00:17:03.090 --> 00:17:07.509
in July about how the exec
intend to approach that.

00:17:07.509 --> 00:17:09.690
But the fact that it's acknowledged

00:17:09.690 --> 00:17:13.620
and it's being lent into again,
we were very supportive of.

00:17:13.620 --> 00:17:15.147
- Okay, thank you.

00:17:15.147 --> 00:17:16.500
Any questions, comments, Elizabeth?

00:17:16.500 --> 00:17:19.740
- Yeah, so it was my
first time at the meeting,

00:17:19.740 --> 00:17:21.780
so Mark has invited me

00:17:21.780 --> 00:17:24.270
and the first half was interesting

00:17:24.270 --> 00:17:27.540
of course to me, but the
second part which was about

00:17:27.540 --> 00:17:28.980
the theory of change and I guess the list

00:17:28.980 --> 00:17:31.720
that Mark's just read out, we could use

00:17:32.781 --> 00:17:36.333
in many of our committees
actually about how we do change.

00:17:37.200 --> 00:17:40.083
For me, it was just basically looking at,

00:17:41.272 --> 00:17:42.105
and I think we discussed,

00:17:42.105 --> 00:17:46.110
we've got highly capable
people out in the service.

00:17:46.110 --> 00:17:50.100
Some of this technology
feels like, why wouldn't you?

00:17:50.100 --> 00:17:50.933
So what is it?

00:17:50.933 --> 00:17:51.766
That's the barrier.

00:17:51.766 --> 00:17:53.160
And I think that's what
we need to come back

00:17:53.160 --> 00:17:54.540
and look at as a team.

00:17:54.540 --> 00:17:56.970
And it's great working with Paul.

00:17:56.970 --> 00:18:00.000
We're looking at and obviously, Rob

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:04.983
about how do we look
at each tech solution,

00:18:05.970 --> 00:18:06.990
not in isolation

00:18:06.990 --> 00:18:09.600
but really look like
about how do we enable it

00:18:09.600 --> 00:18:10.890
and support it and the support

00:18:10.890 --> 00:18:12.600
that adoption out in the service.

00:18:12.600 --> 00:18:14.430
And it needs to be just crosscutting

00:18:14.430 --> 00:18:15.263
through everything we do.

00:18:15.263 --> 00:18:18.510
And I think for me it
was, I came away thinking

00:18:18.510 --> 00:18:21.153
I'd like to be the
Wayfinder sponsor for sure.

00:18:22.350 --> 00:18:23.640
Coming through the CFO route,

00:18:23.640 --> 00:18:27.390
but I think, I'd have to
have a challenge from Paul

00:18:27.390 --> 00:18:28.680
'cause I think he wants to as well now.

00:18:28.680 --> 00:18:31.530
So it was just a really good discussion

00:18:31.530 --> 00:18:34.380
and I would just just like
to say thank you to Mark

00:18:34.380 --> 00:18:35.520
as well for the invitation.

00:18:35.520 --> 00:18:37.800
I'm looking forward to being on the group.

00:18:37.800 --> 00:18:39.360
- Great, thank you, Elizabeth

00:18:39.360 --> 00:18:41.460
and thanks for having that.

00:18:41.460 --> 00:18:43.950
I mean, following up upon that.

00:18:43.950 --> 00:18:44.783
So,

00:18:46.290 --> 00:18:48.750
we can see all this amazing
stuff from tech, you know,

00:18:48.750 --> 00:18:51.767
we've now been using it in
other aspects of our lives

00:18:51.767 --> 00:18:53.820
day to day for, you know,
depending on who you are,

00:18:53.820 --> 00:18:56.040
at least 10 years if not for some people,

00:18:56.040 --> 00:18:57.897
20 years or indeed more.

00:18:57.897 --> 00:19:01.483
And we know it's a continual
source of frustration

00:19:01.483 --> 00:19:04.620
for the wider population,
for our users, for our staff,

00:19:04.620 --> 00:19:06.870
you know, it comes up all the time.

00:19:06.870 --> 00:19:09.387
How do we accelerate progress

00:19:09.387 --> 00:19:13.840
and how do we as a board
increasingly assure ourselves

00:19:13.840 --> 00:19:15.960
that, you know, yes
this is going to be hard

00:19:15.960 --> 00:19:19.609
across such large entities
but we have to accelerate?

00:19:19.609 --> 00:19:24.090
- So I don't think it's
a technology question

00:19:24.090 --> 00:19:27.690
and the technology we're talking about

00:19:27.690 --> 00:19:29.910
is in no way cutting edge.

00:19:29.910 --> 00:19:33.630
So this has been proven across
the economy in your life

00:19:33.630 --> 00:19:36.213
and as you say, we all
use it all the time.

00:19:37.440 --> 00:19:41.460
This is really about large
scale business change

00:19:41.460 --> 00:19:42.660
and operational change.

00:19:42.660 --> 00:19:47.660
And I think the willingness of Rob, Paul

00:19:48.480 --> 00:19:50.580
and Elizabeth to lean into this,

00:19:50.580 --> 00:19:53.670
which is, this isn't what
technology's going to do,

00:19:53.670 --> 00:19:56.910
this is how we, as an
operational leadership,

00:19:56.910 --> 00:20:01.590
are going to make sure the
resources in the right place,

00:20:01.590 --> 00:20:04.590
make sure people have clarity,
make sure they understand

00:20:04.590 --> 00:20:07.380
how to deal with the
trade-offs that will come.

00:20:07.380 --> 00:20:10.320
Because it's hard when
you've got waiting lists

00:20:10.320 --> 00:20:12.057
and you want to put something
in at the same time.

00:20:12.057 --> 00:20:13.500
And I think that can-

00:20:13.500 --> 00:20:15.503
- [Penny] They're synergistic.

00:20:15.503 --> 00:20:17.820
- They're synergistic
over a 12-month period

00:20:17.820 --> 00:20:20.040
but not necessarily a three-month period.

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:22.260
And the pressure the system
is under is definitely

00:20:22.260 --> 00:20:25.010
where people are asking for
month by month improvement.

00:20:28.436 --> 00:20:30.607
For the first time in two years,

00:20:31.980 --> 00:20:33.270
I think the spirit

00:20:33.270 --> 00:20:36.750
and energy with which the
executive are leaning into it

00:20:36.750 --> 00:20:39.120
is a fundamental change.

00:20:39.120 --> 00:20:41.940
And I think as that then
cascades operationally down

00:20:41.940 --> 00:20:45.150
through the trusts and
down into the front line,

00:20:45.150 --> 00:20:46.860
we should start feeling a difference.

00:20:46.860 --> 00:20:48.030
This undoubtedly hard

00:20:48.030 --> 00:20:52.230
and in all large organisations
I've ever been involved with,

00:20:52.230 --> 00:20:56.400
cross-cutting change is a cultural issue

00:20:56.400 --> 00:20:58.100
and it needs to come from the top.

00:20:59.400 --> 00:21:01.200
- So I think we might all ask

00:21:01.200 --> 00:21:03.185
and encourage, well first of
all, thank you for the work

00:21:03.185 --> 00:21:06.300
that you're doing, Mark,
on this and others.

00:21:06.300 --> 00:21:07.713
But we might all collectively ask

00:21:07.713 --> 00:21:12.713
that the Digital Data
and Technology Committee

00:21:12.810 --> 00:21:16.050
continues to ask that question,.

00:21:16.050 --> 00:21:17.100
Why aren't we doing more?

00:21:17.100 --> 00:21:19.770
Why aren't we going further and faster

00:21:19.770 --> 00:21:21.720
and reaping the absolutely fantastic

00:21:21.720 --> 00:21:23.907
benefits that this offers us?

00:21:23.907 --> 00:21:28.563
- And one final point,
what I am fully supportive,

00:21:31.500 --> 00:21:33.960
there are always too many good ideas.

00:21:33.960 --> 00:21:35.850
What we particularly like

00:21:35.850 --> 00:21:39.630
is that the executive have selected

00:21:39.630 --> 00:21:43.620
four very big things
which have very big impact

00:21:43.620 --> 00:21:46.110
and they are going to
prioritise the delivery

00:21:46.110 --> 00:21:49.620
of those knowing that other
things won't get done.

00:21:49.620 --> 00:21:54.030
And one thing, what that
will give us I think

00:21:54.030 --> 00:21:57.090
and more importantly, at
trust level is momentum

00:21:57.090 --> 00:21:59.355
and belief that the
world can be different.

00:21:59.355 --> 00:22:02.520
- Yeah, I hope so.

00:22:02.520 --> 00:22:04.563
In the next 12 months,
which is what you promised.

00:22:05.880 --> 00:22:09.150
Okay, David, Strategy Committee.

00:22:09.150 --> 00:22:13.490
- Yes, so our first paper was on the work

00:22:13.490 --> 00:22:17.070
of the strategy and healthcare
policy directorate itself

00:22:17.070 --> 00:22:22.070
under Paul, including looking
at the pipeline of projects

00:22:24.990 --> 00:22:27.870
that are going on across both NHS England

00:22:27.870 --> 00:22:32.730
and the department, which
is a very long list.

00:22:32.730 --> 00:22:34.320
And one of the things we
are very anxious about

00:22:34.320 --> 00:22:36.030
is to make sure the work's focused

00:22:36.030 --> 00:22:38.733
on the high priority areas,

00:22:39.870 --> 00:22:41.760
that there's a proper governance process

00:22:41.760 --> 00:22:43.560
so that before coming to the board

00:22:43.560 --> 00:22:46.650
or everything goes through
a committee of the board

00:22:46.650 --> 00:22:48.813
or an equivalent governance committee,

00:22:49.920 --> 00:22:53.580
that all of the cross-cutting issues

00:22:53.580 --> 00:22:55.530
and of course, there's a
lot of interrelationship

00:22:55.530 --> 00:23:00.530
between these different
projects is adequately covered.

00:23:00.780 --> 00:23:02.730
And perhaps in some ways,
most important of all

00:23:02.730 --> 00:23:04.980
that we're absolutely
clear about what it is

00:23:04.980 --> 00:23:07.950
that we're trying to get
out of these projects.

00:23:07.950 --> 00:23:10.470
So we spent quite a bit
of time talking about

00:23:10.470 --> 00:23:15.030
how do we make sure that the
work of these project teams,

00:23:15.030 --> 00:23:17.370
study teams working on individual policy

00:23:17.370 --> 00:23:20.700
or strategy areas actually has an impact

00:23:20.700 --> 00:23:24.510
and doesn't have the problem

00:23:24.510 --> 00:23:27.000
that we're also too familiar with,

00:23:27.000 --> 00:23:30.330
which is that a very large
report is produced possibly

00:23:30.330 --> 00:23:32.790
with a several hundred recommendations

00:23:32.790 --> 00:23:34.690
and then it sits on a shelf somewhere.

00:23:35.580 --> 00:23:37.260
So we spend quite a
bit of time about that.

00:23:37.260 --> 00:23:39.360
I think we are getting to
grips with all of this,

00:23:39.360 --> 00:23:40.830
but there is a lot going on

00:23:40.830 --> 00:23:44.163
and it is a very complex scene.

00:23:45.120 --> 00:23:47.250
But we're getting there, I think.

00:23:47.250 --> 00:23:51.330
We also talked about which of
these many projects should we,

00:23:51.330 --> 00:23:54.960
in the Strategy Committee, be
particularly concerned about.

00:23:54.960 --> 00:23:57.060
So many of them will
get reviews, as I say,

00:23:57.060 --> 00:23:59.763
by other committees of
the board and elsewhere.

00:24:00.690 --> 00:24:02.430
But we want to focus first

00:24:02.430 --> 00:24:04.740
of all on the big service
transformation projects.

00:24:04.740 --> 00:24:07.680
So things like the urgent

00:24:07.680 --> 00:24:10.743
and emergency care, elective care,

00:24:11.580 --> 00:24:14.477
mental health strategy,
neighbourhoods and so on.

00:24:14.477 --> 00:24:18.720
So the things that really are
about transforming the way

00:24:18.720 --> 00:24:21.600
the NHS delivers its services.

00:24:21.600 --> 00:24:24.150
Then secondly, the sort
of financial underpinnings

00:24:24.150 --> 00:24:25.350
of all of this.

00:24:25.350 --> 00:24:30.060
So payment strategy in
close cooperation of course

00:24:30.060 --> 00:24:31.593
with the Finance Committee.

00:24:33.540 --> 00:24:38.540
Capital strategy and what all
of this means for productivity

00:24:38.640 --> 00:24:41.730
and then one final topic,

00:24:41.730 --> 00:24:43.260
which is how it all knits together.

00:24:43.260 --> 00:24:47.760
How is this all going
to generate the changes

00:24:47.760 --> 00:24:49.500
that are described in 10-year health plan,

00:24:49.500 --> 00:24:52.413
which is fundamentally
what it's all about.

00:24:53.730 --> 00:24:56.040
So that was our first topic.

00:24:56.040 --> 00:24:59.403
Then we moved on to two pieces of work

00:24:59.403 --> 00:25:01.560
that are actually ongoing.

00:25:01.560 --> 00:25:02.910
One on market management.

00:25:02.910 --> 00:25:06.600
So in many areas of service delivery,

00:25:06.600 --> 00:25:10.140
we have a sort of mixed
market of delivery, some of it

00:25:10.140 --> 00:25:15.140
provided by independent
suppliers, some of it by the NHS.

00:25:16.080 --> 00:25:20.820
These markets don't always
result in the best value

00:25:20.820 --> 00:25:23.730
for money for the NHS.

00:25:23.730 --> 00:25:26.430
Maybe there can even be issues

00:25:26.430 --> 00:25:27.960
around quality of provision.

00:25:27.960 --> 00:25:32.190
So we have to worry about how
those markets are managed.

00:25:32.190 --> 00:25:34.540
Fundamentally, that's
the job of commissioners,

00:25:36.090 --> 00:25:38.280
but commissioners need help

00:25:38.280 --> 00:25:39.690
to understand how to deal with this.

00:25:39.690 --> 00:25:42.300
So this is about understanding
what are the sorts

00:25:42.300 --> 00:25:43.980
of issues we're facing,

00:25:43.980 --> 00:25:46.980
particularly which markets
are we facing them in,

00:25:46.980 --> 00:25:49.980
how do we address those issues

00:25:49.980 --> 00:25:54.743
and how do we help commissioners
on an ongoing basis manage

00:25:55.800 --> 00:25:57.600
and deal with those sorts of issues.

00:25:58.560 --> 00:26:01.920
So that was first topic.

00:26:01.920 --> 00:26:05.040
Then we talked about
the work on the urgent

00:26:05.040 --> 00:26:06.663
and emergency care pathway.

00:26:09.300 --> 00:26:11.190
So this is clearly central to

00:26:11.190 --> 00:26:14.950
the big changes in the 10-year health plan

00:26:16.920 --> 00:26:20.643
as a fundamental redesign of
how services are provided.

00:26:21.600 --> 00:26:24.330
So we were looking at where we've got to

00:26:24.330 --> 00:26:27.750
in terms of the specific sorts of changes

00:26:27.750 --> 00:26:30.570
that we're believe need to be made

00:26:30.570 --> 00:26:33.930
and what that implies for resources, human

00:26:33.930 --> 00:26:38.220
and financial resources across the system.

00:26:38.220 --> 00:26:41.310
So that was a view of progress.

00:26:41.310 --> 00:26:42.720
We did also have a discussion

00:26:42.720 --> 00:26:45.420
about the mental health strategy,

00:26:45.420 --> 00:26:47.670
which is in the early stages.

00:26:47.670 --> 00:26:49.990
So this was mostly a discussion about

00:26:51.000 --> 00:26:53.340
what the team is going to be doing.

00:26:53.340 --> 00:26:56.460
It is taking a lot of work
that's already been done.

00:26:56.460 --> 00:26:58.710
So there's the review into NEETs,

00:26:58.710 --> 00:27:01.320
which we heard about very recently,

00:27:01.320 --> 00:27:04.203
the review into prevalence
and support of mental health.

00:27:05.250 --> 00:27:07.950
There's been a deep dive
into the supply side

00:27:07.950 --> 00:27:10.020
and we have a modern service framework.

00:27:10.020 --> 00:27:12.960
So the question is how do
you pull all this together

00:27:12.960 --> 00:27:15.960
into an overall strategy?

00:27:15.960 --> 00:27:20.960
And again, our focus was
on what are the big ideas,

00:27:21.960 --> 00:27:25.260
the big moves that might
come out of this work

00:27:25.260 --> 00:27:27.873
that will have a real
impact on the ground.

00:27:28.800 --> 00:27:31.260
But as I say that this is early work

00:27:31.260 --> 00:27:36.150
and so this was about getting it set off

00:27:36.150 --> 00:27:37.383
in the right direction.

00:27:38.550 --> 00:27:39.523
And that was it.

00:27:40.842 --> 00:27:41.675
- Thank you.

00:27:41.675 --> 00:27:45.120
Any other comments, questions?

00:27:45.120 --> 00:27:45.953
Okay, Paul.

00:27:45.953 --> 00:27:47.880
- One quick addition.

00:27:47.880 --> 00:27:49.079
I think the first part

00:27:49.079 --> 00:27:50.130
of the discussion was really important

00:27:50.130 --> 00:27:51.810
and relates to a discussion
we've been having

00:27:51.810 --> 00:27:53.130
at executive level,

00:27:53.130 --> 00:27:55.500
a broader discussion
about how we communicate

00:27:55.500 --> 00:27:56.910
with the service in the most effective

00:27:56.910 --> 00:27:58.740
and supportive and helpful way.

00:27:58.740 --> 00:28:01.770
And I think what's implicit
in David's initial remarks

00:28:01.770 --> 00:28:04.050
is that we've got too many pieces of work

00:28:04.050 --> 00:28:05.460
that are called strategies

00:28:05.460 --> 00:28:08.610
and think where teams think
a strategy is what's needed

00:28:08.610 --> 00:28:09.810
and what is going to move the dial

00:28:09.810 --> 00:28:11.610
and what's happening out in the service.

00:28:11.610 --> 00:28:12.870
And I think actually what we've learned

00:28:12.870 --> 00:28:14.100
and we discuss this a lot with Rob

00:28:14.100 --> 00:28:16.410
and other colleagues, is
actually sometimes we need

00:28:16.410 --> 00:28:20.670
to be a lot more agile, a lot
more direct, a lot more quick

00:28:20.670 --> 00:28:22.080
on our feet in terms of thinking actually

00:28:22.080 --> 00:28:24.210
what are the other ways in
which we can communicate

00:28:24.210 --> 00:28:26.010
without waiting for a
six-month piece of work.

00:28:26.010 --> 00:28:27.810
Sometimes those pieces of work are needed

00:28:27.810 --> 00:28:30.270
because of the scale and complexity

00:28:30.270 --> 00:28:31.950
and change that's implied

00:28:31.950 --> 00:28:33.240
and the examples David spoke about

00:28:33.240 --> 00:28:34.740
I think are good examples.

00:28:34.740 --> 00:28:36.870
But there are others where
we've got too much work going on

00:28:36.870 --> 00:28:39.420
to produce documents where
instead, we should find other

00:28:39.420 --> 00:28:41.160
and more direct ways to have conversations

00:28:41.160 --> 00:28:43.170
with the service about
what we think they need

00:28:43.170 --> 00:28:45.060
and how we can help them.

00:28:45.060 --> 00:28:45.960
- Okay.

00:28:45.960 --> 00:28:48.060
One thing I might just add

00:28:48.060 --> 00:28:51.720
'cause I'm sure some people
listening will be interested is,

00:28:51.720 --> 00:28:54.130
are through these value

00:28:55.500 --> 00:28:57.570
whole-scale strategic
reviews, in particular,

00:28:57.570 --> 00:28:58.403
the mental health.

00:28:58.403 --> 00:29:01.350
Absolutely, working with
communities, with users,

00:29:01.350 --> 00:29:04.290
with clinicians, frontline
staff and so on to do that work.

00:29:04.290 --> 00:29:06.240
So slight risk it comes over

00:29:06.240 --> 00:29:08.400
as it's sort of three people
locked in a darkened room

00:29:08.400 --> 00:29:09.450
but actually that these pieces

00:29:09.450 --> 00:29:11.250
of work are much broader than that.

00:29:11.250 --> 00:29:12.330
- Yeah, well in fact, of course,

00:29:12.330 --> 00:29:13.560
that was exactly the sort of topic

00:29:13.560 --> 00:29:15.300
we were talking about when we were asking

00:29:15.300 --> 00:29:16.320
in a generic sense,

00:29:16.320 --> 00:29:18.690
how do we make sure this work has impact?

00:29:18.690 --> 00:29:21.327
And part of the answer is
that it is done with people

00:29:21.327 --> 00:29:23.498
who are actually going
to have to implement it.

00:29:23.498 --> 00:29:26.801
So that it is based in
reality from the outset,

00:29:26.801 --> 00:29:28.980
absolutely, yeah.

00:29:28.980 --> 00:29:30.270
- Okay.

00:29:30.270 --> 00:29:31.350
So and we're just on

00:29:31.350 --> 00:29:33.660
to our Audit and Risk Assurance Committee,

00:29:33.660 --> 00:29:35.743
so Sarah-Jane, over to you again.

00:29:35.743 --> 00:29:37.392
- I'm doing Jeremy as well as Louise.

00:29:37.392 --> 00:29:41.520
So I've got, yeah, it's two
individual sets meetings

00:29:41.520 --> 00:29:42.353
to report back on.

00:29:42.353 --> 00:29:44.400
So the first of those is the meeting

00:29:44.400 --> 00:29:45.540
actually took place yesterday.

00:29:45.540 --> 00:29:49.986
We had a joint DHSE and NHSE
Audit and Risk Committee

00:29:49.986 --> 00:29:51.420
where the main topic

00:29:51.420 --> 00:29:54.963
of conversation was the
transition governance and risk.

00:29:56.790 --> 00:29:59.730
We started off by considering
an internal audit review

00:29:59.730 --> 00:30:03.840
of the governance processes
around the transition process

00:30:03.840 --> 00:30:05.760
and the deputy SRO for the programme,

00:30:05.760 --> 00:30:08.790
Emma Reed came to the committee
to update us on progress

00:30:08.790 --> 00:30:11.040
since that audit was undertaken.

00:30:11.040 --> 00:30:14.310
It's clear a significant
amount of work has taken place.

00:30:14.310 --> 00:30:15.600
I think the committee really wanted

00:30:15.600 --> 00:30:18.720
to recognise the seriousness
with which the audit

00:30:18.720 --> 00:30:20.700
had been taken by the team.

00:30:20.700 --> 00:30:23.280
But there were still some
quite significant concerns

00:30:23.280 --> 00:30:24.900
that were raised, particularly given

00:30:24.900 --> 00:30:29.900
that we are 10 months away
now from the day one go live.

00:30:30.030 --> 00:30:33.120
And the critical path has
a lot of interdependencies

00:30:33.120 --> 00:30:36.600
and any delay causes a
significant amount of risk.

00:30:36.600 --> 00:30:40.290
In particular, the
forthcoming publish location

00:30:40.290 --> 00:30:42.003
of the target operating model.

00:30:42.960 --> 00:30:45.270
Really, the committee was
really very keen to see

00:30:45.270 --> 00:30:47.550
that published as soon as possible

00:30:47.550 --> 00:30:50.310
and that the approach around
the appointment of roles

00:30:50.310 --> 00:30:53.580
and the reward strategies
for those were also clear

00:30:53.580 --> 00:30:55.770
so that people have got the certainty

00:30:55.770 --> 00:30:59.100
and we've got people in
position in critical roles

00:30:59.100 --> 00:31:00.513
as we move forwards.

00:31:03.030 --> 00:31:04.560
Obviously, we've got colleagues,

00:31:04.560 --> 00:31:06.960
Mark was there may wish to add

00:31:06.960 --> 00:31:11.960
but drew some analogies with
mergers in the corporate world

00:31:12.090 --> 00:31:15.090
where even when everything happens in line

00:31:15.090 --> 00:31:18.930
with the timescales and
timetables that are set out,

00:31:18.930 --> 00:31:22.500
there is still significant
issues at the point of go live

00:31:22.500 --> 00:31:25.710
and many are not able to
actually deliver on the benefits

00:31:25.710 --> 00:31:28.350
and that we needed to take
each of these individual steps

00:31:28.350 --> 00:31:30.990
and components exceptionally seriously.

00:31:30.990 --> 00:31:33.240
And whilst we did have
a long discussion about

00:31:33.240 --> 00:31:36.630
how we might mitigate day one risk,

00:31:36.630 --> 00:31:38.520
the committee was very
clear that it wanted

00:31:38.520 --> 00:31:40.650
to do everything it could
to meet the timescales

00:31:40.650 --> 00:31:43.620
to avoid that from happening.

00:31:43.620 --> 00:31:45.630
So I think, yeah, as I say,

00:31:45.630 --> 00:31:48.090
although progress has
been made, there are lots

00:31:48.090 --> 00:31:49.320
of outstanding risks

00:31:49.320 --> 00:31:51.660
and although the joint
committee was not due

00:31:51.660 --> 00:31:54.900
to meet again till October, it was agreed

00:31:54.900 --> 00:31:57.270
that actually we should
meet in advance of that

00:31:57.270 --> 00:32:00.750
over the summer to check in on progress.

00:32:00.750 --> 00:32:02.910
And maybe I'll pause there, Penny,

00:32:02.910 --> 00:32:05.550
before I go to the NHS England only one,

00:32:05.550 --> 00:32:08.790
I don't know whether Mark
wishes to add 'cause he was.

00:32:08.790 --> 00:32:10.110
- Well it was an observation

00:32:10.110 --> 00:32:15.110
which is in much smaller organisations

00:32:15.690 --> 00:32:18.300
and we were talking about Jeremy

00:32:18.300 --> 00:32:21.480
and I clearly have
exposure to corporate world

00:32:21.480 --> 00:32:26.140
merger in acquisitions of
much smaller scale than this

00:32:27.030 --> 00:32:32.030
consistently fail with longer
to do it and more detail.

00:32:34.650 --> 00:32:38.400
And therefore, I think
it's relatively easy

00:32:38.400 --> 00:32:40.980
to underestimate the
things we don't know yet

00:32:40.980 --> 00:32:42.660
and how hard it's going to manage.

00:32:42.660 --> 00:32:46.380
And the reality is we operationally run

00:32:46.380 --> 00:32:50.670
a very, very large system where funds need

00:32:50.670 --> 00:32:52.830
to be flowing on the 1st of April.

00:32:52.830 --> 00:32:55.620
So I think we just need to be super aware

00:32:55.620 --> 00:32:57.810
of whether we are hitting the timetables

00:32:57.810 --> 00:33:00.390
and where the decisions
are being made in time.

00:33:00.390 --> 00:33:03.180
- So Sarah-Jane, I mean, it's
for a slightly wider group

00:33:03.180 --> 00:33:06.090
but my non-exec colleagues have asked

00:33:06.090 --> 00:33:11.070
that we do have a board workshop
explicitly on this I think,

00:33:11.070 --> 00:33:13.927
so that we can all really
understand exactly as you say,

00:33:13.927 --> 00:33:16.920
you know, where we're
at, where the risks are,

00:33:16.920 --> 00:33:18.900
whether we're making
sufficient progress as quickly

00:33:18.900 --> 00:33:21.540
as we would need to see
and in the right direction.

00:33:21.540 --> 00:33:23.650
So we will try and get that scheduled

00:33:24.600 --> 00:33:26.150
probably in the next few weeks.

00:33:27.360 --> 00:33:28.380
- Okay.

00:33:28.380 --> 00:33:30.580
Shall I move on to the
NHS Audit Committee?

00:33:30.580 --> 00:33:34.680
Yeah, so three areas to
pull through from that.

00:33:34.680 --> 00:33:37.020
So I'll finish with the risk management

00:33:37.020 --> 00:33:39.480
'cause that then links to
the risk management paper

00:33:39.480 --> 00:33:40.313
that we've got.

00:33:40.313 --> 00:33:42.690
So we spent a considerable amount

00:33:42.690 --> 00:33:45.990
of time looking over our
internal audit reviews

00:33:45.990 --> 00:33:48.840
and in particular some
of our control processes.

00:33:48.840 --> 00:33:51.600
There has been a recent internal audit

00:33:51.600 --> 00:33:54.420
where there has been some
breach around internal controls

00:33:54.420 --> 00:33:56.070
and contract management processes.

00:33:56.070 --> 00:34:00.390
So the relevant exec team
members are addressing that

00:34:00.390 --> 00:34:02.220
in the particular instance.

00:34:02.220 --> 00:34:05.250
But I think a strong
sense that we as a board

00:34:05.250 --> 00:34:08.490
and as the exec team
need to be reinforcing

00:34:08.490 --> 00:34:11.520
that even when we've got
quite challenging things

00:34:11.520 --> 00:34:12.750
that we need to deliver on

00:34:12.750 --> 00:34:15.660
and delivery is important, that a breach

00:34:15.660 --> 00:34:19.680
of controls is not an acceptable
under any circumstance.

00:34:19.680 --> 00:34:22.530
So I think some specific things to address

00:34:22.530 --> 00:34:24.060
from that particular audit.

00:34:24.060 --> 00:34:25.890
But just to really going back and ensuring

00:34:25.890 --> 00:34:27.360
and strengthening that we are following

00:34:27.360 --> 00:34:30.510
all of our internal process and controls.

00:34:30.510 --> 00:34:33.240
We also spent some time talking
about the future services

00:34:33.240 --> 00:34:36.180
programme, which is under the
leadership of Amanda Doyle

00:34:36.180 --> 00:34:39.630
and Michelle Kane as
the programme director.

00:34:39.630 --> 00:34:43.230
This is surrounding the digital
services provided to GPs

00:34:43.230 --> 00:34:45.210
and a system that needs to be replaced

00:34:45.210 --> 00:34:47.280
during the next three to four years.

00:34:47.280 --> 00:34:48.420
There is a lot of work to do

00:34:48.420 --> 00:34:50.760
to get this right is obviously
absolutely fundamental

00:34:50.760 --> 00:34:52.410
to the underpinnings of our strategy

00:34:52.410 --> 00:34:54.540
around neighbourhood health and so on.

00:34:54.540 --> 00:34:56.430
So there's a lot to do
but I think the committee

00:34:56.430 --> 00:34:59.310
were really wanted to note the progress

00:34:59.310 --> 00:35:01.530
that has been made I think
since Michelle has taken

00:35:01.530 --> 00:35:03.093
on that role.

00:35:04.200 --> 00:35:05.850
Then overall on risk management,

00:35:05.850 --> 00:35:07.410
I'll come onto this on the paper,

00:35:07.410 --> 00:35:11.187
there was a think a little
bit of challenge from,

00:35:11.187 --> 00:35:13.560
not a little bit, there
was a lot of challenge

00:35:13.560 --> 00:35:15.570
from non-executive colleagues

00:35:15.570 --> 00:35:19.470
that the risk paper had
made perhaps too much

00:35:19.470 --> 00:35:22.350
of an optimistic assessment
about the progress

00:35:22.350 --> 00:35:25.290
that had been made about
against some of our risk areas,

00:35:25.290 --> 00:35:27.690
which I'll come onto in
the paper at the moment.

00:35:28.811 --> 00:35:32.760
But it then led to a discussion
about what are the things

00:35:32.760 --> 00:35:37.760
that the ARAC committee
is most concerned about.

00:35:38.370 --> 00:35:41.400
And I think there were four areas.

00:35:41.400 --> 00:35:43.620
The first of that is cyber risk,

00:35:43.620 --> 00:35:46.170
which Mark has just talked about.

00:35:46.170 --> 00:35:48.450
The second was the
future services programme

00:35:48.450 --> 00:35:51.413
given there's a lot of
work that we need to do

00:35:51.413 --> 00:35:53.250
quite quickly on that.

00:35:53.250 --> 00:35:54.930
The third was the transformation programme

00:35:54.930 --> 00:35:57.660
we just talked about and
then the fourth link back

00:35:57.660 --> 00:35:59.340
to what Elizabeth was just talking about

00:35:59.340 --> 00:36:00.990
around the theory of change.

00:36:00.990 --> 00:36:04.050
So we spend a lot of time talking
about our what type risks,

00:36:04.050 --> 00:36:07.860
but do we spend enough
talking about our how risks.

00:36:07.860 --> 00:36:10.740
So the committee have asked me
working with exec colleagues

00:36:10.740 --> 00:36:13.350
to think about those
four areas in particular,

00:36:13.350 --> 00:36:15.960
which I think maybe then fits
pending with what you said

00:36:15.960 --> 00:36:18.660
around a workshop on the transition to try

00:36:18.660 --> 00:36:20.910
and strengthen our risk
management approaches

00:36:20.910 --> 00:36:23.070
around all of those four.

00:36:23.070 --> 00:36:26.340
So we will do some work
on that moving forwards.

00:36:26.340 --> 00:36:29.100
Which probably then goes onto
the risk management paper

00:36:29.100 --> 00:36:30.287
if that's okay.

00:36:30.287 --> 00:36:32.463
So the on the risk management paper.

00:36:33.600 --> 00:36:36.930
We do feel that we've made
progress against quite a few

00:36:36.930 --> 00:36:38.380
of these areas and I think

00:36:39.776 --> 00:36:42.810
the committee yesterday
there was an acknowledgement

00:36:42.810 --> 00:36:44.730
that there's been a much tighter ownership

00:36:44.730 --> 00:36:46.440
from exec colleagues of the risks

00:36:46.440 --> 00:36:49.920
and that people are putting
in the steps that drive

00:36:49.920 --> 00:36:51.570
the mitigations.

00:36:51.570 --> 00:36:54.060
But we still are left
with a range of things

00:36:54.060 --> 00:36:57.240
that we don't feel are in
a tolerable sort of risk

00:36:57.240 --> 00:36:59.700
with a tolerable risk
score principally cyber

00:36:59.700 --> 00:37:01.200
but also a few other things.

00:37:01.200 --> 00:37:02.850
So there's a few specifics in the paper.

00:37:02.850 --> 00:37:06.150
I think on the strategic
risks, we have made quite a lot

00:37:06.150 --> 00:37:08.400
of progress as David set
out in the committee.

00:37:08.400 --> 00:37:10.740
We are taking more of
those joined up steps

00:37:10.740 --> 00:37:13.890
and sort of focusing on the areas

00:37:13.890 --> 00:37:16.470
that we really know will
be in service of the NHS.

00:37:16.470 --> 00:37:19.020
But on the operational
risk side, we're still left

00:37:19.020 --> 00:37:21.390
with a range of challenging areas.

00:37:21.390 --> 00:37:24.690
If you look on page 23 of the board pack,

00:37:24.690 --> 00:37:28.710
we've had a few areas that
are still a risk score of 25.

00:37:28.710 --> 00:37:33.150
So adult secure inpatient
capacity in particular

00:37:33.150 --> 00:37:35.547
because some of the issues that we've had

00:37:35.547 --> 00:37:36.390
at St. Andrew's Healthcare

00:37:36.390 --> 00:37:38.460
an already very challenging environment

00:37:38.460 --> 00:37:42.030
from a capacity perspective
has become more challenging.

00:37:42.030 --> 00:37:45.570
Digital workforce capacity
based on the amount of change

00:37:45.570 --> 00:37:48.180
that Mark describes needs to take place

00:37:48.180 --> 00:37:49.830
and the alignment between the capacity

00:37:49.830 --> 00:37:53.340
and capability that we've got
and the job we need to do.

00:37:53.340 --> 00:37:56.680
There's an ongoing issue with
the IT system that supports

00:37:57.960 --> 00:38:02.460
the oversight of performance
issues in general practise

00:38:02.460 --> 00:38:05.160
where the system isn't fit for purpose,

00:38:05.160 --> 00:38:08.880
which is having an impact on
the regional team's oversight

00:38:08.880 --> 00:38:11.940
and then the cybersecurity
and resilience risk as well.

00:38:11.940 --> 00:38:15.895
So they are things that we need
to still continue, you know,

00:38:15.895 --> 00:38:17.940
to work very closely on.

00:38:17.940 --> 00:38:20.220
There was just then one specific I think

00:38:20.220 --> 00:38:23.220
that the board asked us to
re-look at last time around

00:38:23.220 --> 00:38:27.390
after we considered the EPRR paper.

00:38:27.390 --> 00:38:29.015
- [Penny] What's that stand for?

00:38:29.015 --> 00:38:31.950
- The Emergency Preparedness
Resilience and Response

00:38:31.950 --> 00:38:33.480
annual report.

00:38:33.480 --> 00:38:35.700
There was, if you record a discussion

00:38:35.700 --> 00:38:40.020
that we had the highest risk
on that as pandemic response

00:38:40.020 --> 00:38:41.160
because it reads over

00:38:41.160 --> 00:38:44.460
to the government's
national risk register.

00:38:44.460 --> 00:38:47.340
Whereas for the NHS, the cyber response

00:38:47.340 --> 00:38:49.170
would be considered to be higher.

00:38:49.170 --> 00:38:51.030
So we've reflected that now

00:38:51.030 --> 00:38:52.440
in the risk registers going forward.

00:38:52.440 --> 00:38:56.730
So obviously, pandemic
planning is still a huge area

00:38:56.730 --> 00:38:58.320
of importance and is very clearly

00:38:58.320 --> 00:39:00.360
so on the operational risk register.

00:39:00.360 --> 00:39:05.360
But we've added more in
there around cyber risk

00:39:05.790 --> 00:39:09.930
and also then, we've added
more around the sort of change

00:39:09.930 --> 00:39:12.210
in environment with
international conflict as well

00:39:12.210 --> 00:39:15.240
and what that potentially
impacts on finance, supply chain,

00:39:15.240 --> 00:39:16.290
and cyber attacks.

00:39:16.290 --> 00:39:20.400
So hopefully that reflects
what the board said

00:39:20.400 --> 00:39:22.980
and we've got the
pandemic plan in part now

00:39:22.980 --> 00:39:24.390
in the correct balance I guess.

00:39:24.390 --> 00:39:27.120
So it's obviously something
that's very important to us

00:39:27.120 --> 00:39:30.333
but it's reflected more
accurately in the risk hierarchy.

00:39:31.470 --> 00:39:32.487
- Thank you.

00:39:32.487 --> 00:39:35.580
- And further to the board
meeting we had in Bournemouth,

00:39:35.580 --> 00:39:38.340
there is going to be a resilience exercise

00:39:38.340 --> 00:39:42.120
on a national basis assuming a full outage

00:39:42.120 --> 00:39:44.340
so that we can really
test business continuity,

00:39:44.340 --> 00:39:47.163
which was one of the
key board requests and.

00:39:49.934 --> 00:39:52.350
- Okay, any comments?

00:39:52.350 --> 00:39:56.313
It's just one from me, which
is, which you've, you know,

00:39:56.313 --> 00:39:59.550
referred to a couple of times,
which is the whole theory

00:39:59.550 --> 00:40:01.143
of change/improvement.

00:40:02.110 --> 00:40:06.120
We've got a big agenda as we know.

00:40:06.120 --> 00:40:07.170
There's lots of things

00:40:07.170 --> 00:40:09.180
that we can do and we want to do that.

00:40:09.180 --> 00:40:12.000
We all know the challenge
really is how do we get it

00:40:12.000 --> 00:40:14.040
to happen on the ground.

00:40:14.040 --> 00:40:16.020
And I'm not saying to answer that now,

00:40:16.020 --> 00:40:18.670
but that does feel to me
like something that we need

00:40:19.524 --> 00:40:21.690
to come back to and have
a more substantive bit.

00:40:21.690 --> 00:40:23.340
We've got our operating model changes,

00:40:23.340 --> 00:40:25.560
but it's also about how do
we get the right balance

00:40:25.560 --> 00:40:28.890
of support to the system out there.

00:40:28.890 --> 00:40:29.723
Paul? Yeah.

00:40:29.723 --> 00:40:32.370
- Just a flag I think on that
we're explicitly planning

00:40:32.370 --> 00:40:34.411
to bring that into the discussion

00:40:34.411 --> 00:40:36.570
that we've got the board
strategy away day in July.

00:40:36.570 --> 00:40:38.280
And I think there we will be reflecting

00:40:38.280 --> 00:40:40.869
on the various things that we've
got in our kind of toolbox.

00:40:40.869 --> 00:40:42.360
And how we organise

00:40:42.360 --> 00:40:44.610
and deploy those things
in a much more joined up

00:40:44.610 --> 00:40:46.380
and coherent and structured way

00:40:46.380 --> 00:40:48.120
across the various
priorities that we've got.

00:40:48.120 --> 00:40:49.067
So hopefully, we're having

00:40:49.067 --> 00:40:51.000
a very explicit discussion about that.

00:40:51.000 --> 00:40:53.070
- I look forward to risk assessing them.

00:40:53.070 --> 00:40:55.800
Good.

00:40:55.800 --> 00:40:59.070
- Okay, I'm going to
move on to Jim's update.

00:40:59.070 --> 00:40:59.903
- Thanks, Penny.

00:40:59.903 --> 00:41:01.050
So I'll pick up on some of the things

00:41:01.050 --> 00:41:02.850
that have already been
said, but first of all,

00:41:02.850 --> 00:41:04.350
when we met last time,

00:41:04.350 --> 00:41:06.930
we were right at the
year end we're watching

00:41:06.930 --> 00:41:09.330
the management information,
very anxious about

00:41:09.330 --> 00:41:11.430
where we would land on
electives for example

00:41:11.430 --> 00:41:14.242
and category two performance and UEC.

00:41:14.242 --> 00:41:15.300
And I think colleagues will have seen

00:41:15.300 --> 00:41:17.820
and there was watching
will have seen the NHS

00:41:17.820 --> 00:41:20.370
did an absolutely unbelievable job to get

00:41:20.370 --> 00:41:21.510
where we needed to be,

00:41:21.510 --> 00:41:23.190
not where we want to be forever.

00:41:23.190 --> 00:41:25.140
Clearly, still a lot of improvement

00:41:25.140 --> 00:41:28.350
but to get overlain on the RTT objective

00:41:28.350 --> 00:41:30.120
and the category two performance

00:41:30.120 --> 00:41:31.890
and various other metrics,

00:41:31.890 --> 00:41:33.840
I think it was a really fantastic job

00:41:33.840 --> 00:41:36.717
by everybody in the NHS
and colleagues here as well

00:41:36.717 --> 00:41:38.130
and their contribution to it.

00:41:38.130 --> 00:41:40.380
So just wanted to record that.

00:41:40.380 --> 00:41:43.110
Some of that's continued
into the financial year.

00:41:43.110 --> 00:41:45.870
So the latest management
information shows continued

00:41:45.870 --> 00:41:48.878
strong performance again
on some of those metrics,

00:41:48.878 --> 00:41:52.740
especially on RTT performance.

00:41:52.740 --> 00:41:55.770
Second point is, you can see
there's different people here.

00:41:55.770 --> 00:41:57.360
So there's fewer people in the room.

00:41:57.360 --> 00:41:59.858
There's people are continuing

00:41:59.858 --> 00:42:03.870
to transition back out into their old jobs

00:42:03.870 --> 00:42:06.310
and normal jobs out of the hostage scheme

00:42:07.455 --> 00:42:08.940
and new hostages are arriving.

00:42:08.940 --> 00:42:11.820
We've made some appointments
who are currently moving

00:42:11.820 --> 00:42:15.870
through the well-oiled machine
that is the approval process.

00:42:15.870 --> 00:42:17.970
So hopefully, we'll announce them soon.

00:42:17.970 --> 00:42:19.650
And we're about to recruit

00:42:19.650 --> 00:42:21.827
to the patient experience director role.

00:42:21.827 --> 00:42:22.660
- Oh wow.

00:42:22.660 --> 00:42:24.090
- Which is a really big step for us.

00:42:24.090 --> 00:42:25.710
We've all been pushing very hard for that

00:42:25.710 --> 00:42:26.970
over a long period of time,

00:42:26.970 --> 00:42:29.490
so I think a big step, but just again,

00:42:29.490 --> 00:42:31.590
and I'm worried about who I
might miss out in all this.

00:42:31.590 --> 00:42:35.790
Thanks to Claire and Meghana
for fulfilling the role

00:42:35.790 --> 00:42:38.610
of medical director so
well in the last year

00:42:38.610 --> 00:42:41.670
and welcome Frankie as well, and Rob.

00:42:41.670 --> 00:42:43.710
Jules isn't here today,

00:42:43.710 --> 00:42:48.710
obviously, he's now moved
on into the pastures new.

00:42:49.170 --> 00:42:52.410
Glen continues in a new
role in the hostage scheme

00:42:52.410 --> 00:42:56.010
as does Meghana in the
deputy chief exec role.

00:42:56.010 --> 00:42:57.660
So a lot of change

00:42:57.660 --> 00:42:59.975
and I'll come back to that
as we're talking about

00:42:59.975 --> 00:43:01.290
the sort of the transformation process

00:43:01.290 --> 00:43:02.860
and integration with DHSC.

00:43:02.860 --> 00:43:05.220
We've also had a political
change with change

00:43:05.220 --> 00:43:06.180
of Secretary of State

00:43:06.180 --> 00:43:09.930
and those of us who work closely with Wes

00:43:09.930 --> 00:43:12.300
will know that he was fantastic working

00:43:12.300 --> 00:43:13.650
really closely with us.

00:43:13.650 --> 00:43:16.380
Very passionate about
improvement for the service,

00:43:16.380 --> 00:43:19.320
really challenged us and
great Secretary of State

00:43:19.320 --> 00:43:22.083
and James, new Secretary of
State landed really well.

00:43:23.010 --> 00:43:24.510
You know, he's put a lot of time

00:43:24.510 --> 00:43:25.830
and effort in really quickly.

00:43:25.830 --> 00:43:27.810
He's all over a very complicated brief.

00:43:27.810 --> 00:43:30.270
Did a great session with chief
execs the other day as well.

00:43:30.270 --> 00:43:32.280
So very, very good start.

00:43:32.280 --> 00:43:34.080
These change processes are complicated

00:43:34.080 --> 00:43:37.102
but it's being managed very
well, which is just as well

00:43:37.102 --> 00:43:38.552
'cause we've got a lot to do.

00:43:39.810 --> 00:43:44.810
Today, the Lord Mann's
review will be published

00:43:44.820 --> 00:43:48.691
in a really horrible state
of affairs where sadly,

00:43:48.691 --> 00:43:52.230
we've all become used to having to cope

00:43:52.230 --> 00:43:55.500
with horrible intolerance.

00:43:55.500 --> 00:43:57.870
People feeling unsafe
in the work environment

00:43:57.870 --> 00:43:59.970
or in a care environment, etc.

00:43:59.970 --> 00:44:02.400
And that's really not what
we want the NHS to be.

00:44:02.400 --> 00:44:05.280
So I think a really good thorough report,

00:44:05.280 --> 00:44:08.910
we will get out the blocks
today with direction

00:44:08.910 --> 00:44:11.097
to the NHS in terms of
how we respond to that.

00:44:11.097 --> 00:44:14.310
And I think really, really
need everybody to step up

00:44:14.310 --> 00:44:18.480
on that, to get to
eradicate as best we can

00:44:18.480 --> 00:44:20.550
this horrible feeling of intolerance

00:44:20.550 --> 00:44:24.630
and people feeling
undervalued at under threat

00:44:24.630 --> 00:44:26.520
that they might not be
cared for appropriately.

00:44:26.520 --> 00:44:29.460
All those things that have
been described so well

00:44:29.460 --> 00:44:32.343
and we need to deal with effectively.

00:44:33.394 --> 00:44:36.270
We're in a different point

00:44:36.270 --> 00:44:38.550
in industrial action
than we were last time.

00:44:38.550 --> 00:44:40.350
Glen maybe can just update on that.

00:44:40.350 --> 00:44:42.870
So discussions are ongoing we do,

00:44:42.870 --> 00:44:46.740
where we are faced in
one of the set of strikes

00:44:46.740 --> 00:44:48.630
in a week or so's time,

00:44:48.630 --> 00:44:51.161
prepared as well as we usually are

00:44:51.161 --> 00:44:53.400
'cause we have become
very good at managing this

00:44:53.400 --> 00:44:54.330
across the NHS.

00:44:54.330 --> 00:44:57.090
Still hopeful we'll find a way forward.

00:44:57.090 --> 00:44:59.220
We'll bring Glen in when I've finished

00:44:59.220 --> 00:45:01.830
just to top that off.

00:45:01.830 --> 00:45:04.350
And there's a couple of
points I just draw out

00:45:04.350 --> 00:45:05.790
on the points made earlier.

00:45:05.790 --> 00:45:10.790
So Mark made a very, very good
point out about cyber risk.

00:45:10.920 --> 00:45:13.950
And I think we've all been
a bit uncomfortable about

00:45:13.950 --> 00:45:18.950
how well prepared we were
to cope with potential risk.

00:45:20.250 --> 00:45:23.010
But the risk environment has
now changed really dramatically

00:45:23.010 --> 00:45:24.120
and is accelerating.

00:45:24.120 --> 00:45:27.390
So we've had colleagues working on this.

00:45:27.390 --> 00:45:29.700
We're going to hopefully
send something out tomorrow

00:45:29.700 --> 00:45:33.090
to the service to say let's
all just do these basic things

00:45:33.090 --> 00:45:36.960
'cause we're in a very much
different risk environment now.

00:45:36.960 --> 00:45:38.340
We need everyone to pull together.

00:45:38.340 --> 00:45:41.970
We're all tied together and
it's a very connected system.

00:45:41.970 --> 00:45:43.950
So we need to all do what we need to do

00:45:43.950 --> 00:45:47.910
and stay agile to that as things develop.

00:45:47.910 --> 00:45:50.850
And then on the risks from
ARAC and People Committee

00:45:50.850 --> 00:45:55.850
and other discussions, we are
in a naturally awkward stage

00:45:56.070 --> 00:46:00.120
in the integration process with DHSC.

00:46:00.120 --> 00:46:03.150
Where there's not much time
left now about 10 months,

00:46:03.150 --> 00:46:06.180
we've got an awful lot,
still a crack finally.

00:46:06.180 --> 00:46:08.730
Make sure we've got the
right operating model,

00:46:08.730 --> 00:46:10.230
got a high level structure work

00:46:10.230 --> 00:46:13.470
and we've got the right decision
making processes in place

00:46:13.470 --> 00:46:17.730
for operational decisions
versus clear, pure policy

00:46:17.730 --> 00:46:20.190
and policy decisions, etc.

00:46:20.190 --> 00:46:21.630
So that's moving.

00:46:21.630 --> 00:46:23.510
It's difficult but moving

00:46:23.510 --> 00:46:26.790
as was just been described previously.

00:46:26.790 --> 00:46:29.670
A lot of work to do to land that quickly.

00:46:29.670 --> 00:46:32.790
But also, we do a lot
of complicated business.

00:46:32.790 --> 00:46:35.450
So we need, as you've said, I
need to have some board time

00:46:35.450 --> 00:46:38.880
to just understand how
we're managing the risks

00:46:38.880 --> 00:46:41.550
of being able to function
in our last few months.

00:46:41.550 --> 00:46:42.600
Make the decisions

00:46:42.600 --> 00:46:45.330
and provide the assurance
that we need to as a board

00:46:45.330 --> 00:46:49.920
and keep a control of things
and also on abolition,

00:46:49.920 --> 00:46:51.570
make sure we can actually
function probably

00:46:51.570 --> 00:46:55.500
we still pay bills and all
those important things.

00:46:55.500 --> 00:46:57.450
So it does feel like we're
at a really key time.

00:46:57.450 --> 00:47:00.120
There's been a lot of good
sessions this week to try

00:47:00.120 --> 00:47:02.070
and get through those issues.

00:47:02.070 --> 00:47:04.560
I think the next couple of
weeks is really quite crunchy

00:47:04.560 --> 00:47:06.543
to make sure we're all over that.

00:47:07.740 --> 00:47:08.573
- Thank you, Jim.

00:47:08.573 --> 00:47:09.450
Glen, do you want to come in on the?

00:47:09.450 --> 00:47:12.630
- Yeah, could just add on
the resident doctor strikes.

00:47:12.630 --> 00:47:14.280
Obviously, strikes affect patients,

00:47:14.280 --> 00:47:16.770
that's the biggest concern for us,

00:47:16.770 --> 00:47:20.910
but also impact on colleagues
and on our finances.

00:47:20.910 --> 00:47:22.620
And even though the
NHS has got really good

00:47:22.620 --> 00:47:26.220
at managing strikes, we really
do want to get to a position

00:47:26.220 --> 00:47:27.053
where they stop.

00:47:28.230 --> 00:47:30.960
The BMA were prioritised

00:47:30.960 --> 00:47:34.203
in Secretary of State's
meetings when he first arrived.

00:47:35.220 --> 00:47:38.520
And despite meeting with them

00:47:38.520 --> 00:47:40.440
and having a really
constructive discussion

00:47:40.440 --> 00:47:43.230
later on that day, they
clearly decided to announce

00:47:43.230 --> 00:47:46.560
the next round of strikes
before the meeting.

00:47:46.560 --> 00:47:48.630
We're asking him to call them off.

00:47:48.630 --> 00:47:50.100
So they announced them after the meeting

00:47:50.100 --> 00:47:51.630
but they'd set up

00:47:51.630 --> 00:47:54.213
to make those announcements
on the same day.

00:47:55.590 --> 00:47:58.440
So we are in discussion

00:47:58.440 --> 00:48:00.510
with the BMA resident doctors committee

00:48:00.510 --> 00:48:02.133
on a daily basis still.

00:48:03.180 --> 00:48:05.680
We've asked them to call the strikes off

00:48:06.630 --> 00:48:09.813
and we think there's a way through that,

00:48:10.830 --> 00:48:15.830
but we will continue to update board

00:48:16.107 --> 00:48:18.180
and the rest of the
NHS on the consequences

00:48:18.180 --> 00:48:20.940
of what will happen if
those strikes happen

00:48:20.940 --> 00:48:24.360
because it will reduce
the resources available

00:48:24.360 --> 00:48:26.270
to offer a future deal to the BMA

00:48:26.270 --> 00:48:28.740
of the level that we've
been able to offer them.

00:48:28.740 --> 00:48:29.573
- Yeah.

00:48:30.790 --> 00:48:31.743
Okay, Ravi.

00:48:32.970 --> 00:48:34.740
- Just wonder whether you
could tell us more about

00:48:34.740 --> 00:48:37.970
what drove the improvements in outcomes

00:48:37.970 --> 00:48:41.040
and whether that can be sustained

00:48:41.040 --> 00:48:42.900
or even accelerated in
the next coming months.

00:48:42.900 --> 00:48:46.230
- Yeah, great question, Ravi.

00:48:46.230 --> 00:48:48.510
So there's an underlying thing

00:48:48.510 --> 00:48:50.280
where the NHS is actually quite good

00:48:50.280 --> 00:48:52.860
at chasing single-point targets.

00:48:52.860 --> 00:48:56.160
So it's just always been
in our DNA, so that rush

00:48:56.160 --> 00:48:59.940
to the year end over the years
we've been quite good at it.

00:48:59.940 --> 00:49:01.320
What often happens then is there's a bit

00:49:01.320 --> 00:49:02.457
of a collapse straight off

00:49:02.457 --> 00:49:04.470
and we spend a while
getting back on our feet.

00:49:04.470 --> 00:49:08.880
Hopefully, at least selectively,
so far on the RTT metric

00:49:08.880 --> 00:49:10.620
that hasn't happened this time.

00:49:10.620 --> 00:49:12.870
But I think there's a
point about alignment

00:49:12.870 --> 00:49:14.250
and leadership and focus

00:49:14.250 --> 00:49:18.030
where right across the
service from about December,

00:49:18.030 --> 00:49:21.030
January coming out of the
December strikes probably people

00:49:21.030 --> 00:49:23.160
got really focused together.

00:49:23.160 --> 00:49:26.220
And a bit of spirit and joy
out of all that, you know,

00:49:26.220 --> 00:49:28.803
people succeed and seeing what works.

00:49:29.850 --> 00:49:31.500
When we've tried to unpack those

00:49:31.500 --> 00:49:34.230
that have made really big
improvements on four hours,

00:49:34.230 --> 00:49:38.250
for example, on ED or on elective waits,

00:49:38.250 --> 00:49:40.890
there are a good number
we've talked about a lot

00:49:40.890 --> 00:49:43.770
where people made really
very big movements

00:49:43.770 --> 00:49:46.230
over short spaces of time, which destroyed

00:49:46.230 --> 00:49:48.660
all of the pre-existing theory

00:49:48.660 --> 00:49:51.660
about how the system works, etc.

00:49:51.660 --> 00:49:52.530
Two are in the room.

00:49:52.530 --> 00:49:54.540
So Glen and Meghana could talk about

00:49:54.540 --> 00:49:56.220
what they've done in ED.

00:49:56.220 --> 00:49:58.823
Haven't checked this week whether
you've maintained it, but-

00:50:00.317 --> 00:50:01.150
- [Meghana] I'll be happy.

00:50:01.150 --> 00:50:02.816
- Yeah.

00:50:02.816 --> 00:50:05.190
So we're trying to industrialise that

00:50:05.190 --> 00:50:06.060
and share it more.

00:50:06.060 --> 00:50:08.350
We had a discussion this
morning with Mark earlier on

00:50:08.350 --> 00:50:11.200
about how AI might be
able to be brought to use

00:50:12.565 --> 00:50:14.423
to help people share the
learning from all of that.

00:50:14.423 --> 00:50:18.360
There's some common characteristics,
leadership alignment,

00:50:18.360 --> 00:50:22.530
clinicians, managers
together using good data

00:50:22.530 --> 00:50:26.713
and measurement, simple change processes.

00:50:26.713 --> 00:50:28.146
Improvement processes

00:50:28.146 --> 00:50:30.900
PDSA has talked about
a lot rather than like-

00:50:30.900 --> 00:50:31.860
- What's PDSA?

00:50:31.860 --> 00:50:33.365
- Plan, Do, Say, Act,

00:50:33.365 --> 00:50:37.830
rather than a very
complex change processes

00:50:37.830 --> 00:50:40.590
and then celebrating success as well.

00:50:40.590 --> 00:50:43.500
So they are common characteristics.

00:50:43.500 --> 00:50:45.210
There are slightly different things

00:50:45.210 --> 00:50:47.730
that have worked in
different sort of settings

00:50:47.730 --> 00:50:49.590
and we are now, we'll talk about it a bit

00:50:49.590 --> 00:50:50.760
next week at ConfedExpo.

00:50:50.760 --> 00:50:52.390
We're trying to power up

00:50:53.310 --> 00:50:54.990
how does everybody know what's worked

00:50:54.990 --> 00:50:57.210
to kind of get those
things to exist everywhere.

00:50:57.210 --> 00:50:59.100
Whether it's sustainable, you know,

00:50:59.100 --> 00:51:02.820
you can never really go every
day the year at that rate.

00:51:02.820 --> 00:51:04.860
But so far, people do look like

00:51:04.860 --> 00:51:07.083
they've kind of largely kept it going.

00:51:08.160 --> 00:51:13.160
And just on that, we
naturally have, you know,

00:51:13.200 --> 00:51:16.710
thanked people over the last
few weeks for what's happened.

00:51:16.710 --> 00:51:19.980
We are now though nearly
at the end of quarter one.

00:51:19.980 --> 00:51:22.110
So we need a really quickly transition

00:51:22.110 --> 00:51:23.550
into delivery of this year.

00:51:23.550 --> 00:51:26.041
Getting ready for winter,
you know, making sure

00:51:26.041 --> 00:51:27.360
we'll manage it every

00:51:27.360 --> 00:51:29.220
and make the last quarter
a little bit easier

00:51:29.220 --> 00:51:31.110
'cause it's pretty
stressful the last quarter

00:51:31.110 --> 00:51:33.540
of the last financial year.

00:51:33.540 --> 00:51:35.390
- So I'm just going to take two questions,

00:51:35.390 --> 00:51:36.556
well three questions

00:51:36.556 --> 00:51:39.570
but then we're a bit behind
so try and accelerate.

00:51:39.570 --> 00:51:42.900
So I've got David then
Glen and then Duncan.

00:51:42.900 --> 00:51:46.170
- So just very quickly, so
in the context of that, Jim,

00:51:46.170 --> 00:51:49.290
what do you think is the
biggest risk to us being able

00:51:49.290 --> 00:51:51.153
to maintain momentum this year?

00:51:52.170 --> 00:51:53.843
- Let's take the other ones, Glen.

00:51:54.690 --> 00:51:56.940
- Yeah, so one of the
things that I was going

00:51:56.940 --> 00:51:59.433
to pick up is the George
Elliot experience,

00:52:00.813 --> 00:52:02.103
which we have maintained,

00:52:04.650 --> 00:52:07.500
which was a focus on flow
rather than capacity actually.

00:52:07.500 --> 00:52:08.700
And as Jim said, some of it

00:52:08.700 --> 00:52:12.150
was about making simple process changes.

00:52:12.150 --> 00:52:14.070
Some of it was about driving protocols

00:52:14.070 --> 00:52:16.380
but a lot of use of
same day emergency care

00:52:16.380 --> 00:52:18.510
of frailty services.

00:52:18.510 --> 00:52:21.930
And some of it was just a
minor change to opening times

00:52:21.930 --> 00:52:23.313
in order to ensure flow.

00:52:24.780 --> 00:52:27.780
But the other important thing
is just that whole mindset.

00:52:27.780 --> 00:52:31.890
So it was driven by
eradicating corridor care,

00:52:31.890 --> 00:52:35.433
not by necessarily chasing
the four hour standard,

00:52:36.300 --> 00:52:38.250
but by putting those measures in place

00:52:38.250 --> 00:52:40.590
and having protocols in
place to move patients

00:52:40.590 --> 00:52:42.090
through the system effectively

00:52:42.090 --> 00:52:45.060
and to discharge them
as quickly as we could.

00:52:45.060 --> 00:52:47.280
That gained the momentum of the team.

00:52:47.280 --> 00:52:49.810
And so that success kind of built success

00:52:50.700 --> 00:52:52.770
and they've desperately wanted to keep

00:52:52.770 --> 00:52:54.663
all of that going and have done.

00:52:54.663 --> 00:52:56.343
- Good to hear. Thank you.

00:52:57.600 --> 00:52:59.370
Okay, was yours a question.
Meghana, was another-

00:52:59.370 --> 00:53:02.130
- Well again, the example
that Jim quoted. Just wanting

00:53:02.130 --> 00:53:05.760
to say the, I think it's
about aligning the goals

00:53:05.760 --> 00:53:07.494
with the clinical teams.

00:53:07.494 --> 00:53:10.800
And also working with primary care.

00:53:10.800 --> 00:53:15.210
I think that interface across
primary and secondary care

00:53:15.210 --> 00:53:17.280
and working together to
understand what's best

00:53:17.280 --> 00:53:18.720
for the patients that coming in

00:53:18.720 --> 00:53:21.480
or don't come in actually
may makes it work.

00:53:21.480 --> 00:53:23.880
And as Glen said, a lot of activity

00:53:23.880 --> 00:53:26.040
in the same day emergency care.

00:53:26.040 --> 00:53:28.950
and then carrying that through to daily

00:53:28.950 --> 00:53:31.070
literally standard work.

00:53:31.070 --> 00:53:33.570
So all the teams actually know

00:53:33.570 --> 00:53:35.700
how the internal processes work

00:53:35.700 --> 00:53:37.800
and what that leads to is discharges

00:53:37.800 --> 00:53:40.290
before 12 noon patients are, you know,

00:53:40.290 --> 00:53:42.360
going home when they know
when they're going home

00:53:42.360 --> 00:53:44.520
and they expect to go
home and they go home.

00:53:44.520 --> 00:53:47.100
And I think keeping that rhythm going

00:53:47.100 --> 00:53:49.680
across the organisation
was really helpful.

00:53:49.680 --> 00:53:50.513
- Very good.

00:53:50.513 --> 00:53:51.540
Thank you both for sharing those.

00:53:51.540 --> 00:53:52.373
Duncan.

00:53:52.373 --> 00:53:55.269
- Mine's are a industrial
relations comment,

00:53:55.269 --> 00:53:56.190
whether you want to finish this.

00:53:56.190 --> 00:53:57.390
- Well let's take that

00:53:57.390 --> 00:53:59.610
and then maybe take David's question

00:53:59.610 --> 00:54:00.960
which can partly answered.

00:54:00.960 --> 00:54:01.793
- Okay, great.

00:54:01.793 --> 00:54:03.060
I just thought it was
also worth just noting

00:54:03.060 --> 00:54:04.380
the progress that's been made

00:54:04.380 --> 00:54:08.190
with the government's
commitment to nursing

00:54:08.190 --> 00:54:09.540
and fairer play for nursing.

00:54:09.540 --> 00:54:13.680
So Danny and I both wrote out
this week to organisations

00:54:13.680 --> 00:54:16.290
to set the timeline for
achieving the review

00:54:16.290 --> 00:54:19.890
of approximately 180,000 band five nurses

00:54:19.890 --> 00:54:21.270
and reviewing their job roles

00:54:21.270 --> 00:54:23.277
and descriptions by October 28.

00:54:23.277 --> 00:54:26.310
And we've asked trust
to come back by the end

00:54:26.310 --> 00:54:27.720
of July with plans for that.

00:54:27.720 --> 00:54:29.460
But that's good.

00:54:29.460 --> 00:54:30.840
We're making progress on this

00:54:30.840 --> 00:54:32.250
and I think that's really
important to note that

00:54:32.250 --> 00:54:34.950
in the context of wider
industrial relations.

00:54:34.950 --> 00:54:35.970
- Great, thank you.

00:54:35.970 --> 00:54:36.803
Jim.

00:54:36.803 --> 00:54:37.727
- Thanks.

00:54:37.727 --> 00:54:40.020
Yeah, just one note, really
good question about risk then.

00:54:40.020 --> 00:54:42.031
So this could be a two hour response,

00:54:42.031 --> 00:54:43.950
I'll try and do it quickly.

00:54:43.950 --> 00:54:46.049
So there's a risk and a few levels here.

00:54:46.049 --> 00:54:49.650
I think the key risks one is
at a macro level, the interplay

00:54:49.650 --> 00:54:53.970
between the financial position,
industrial action, whether

00:54:53.970 --> 00:54:56.160
that goes on or it gets settled

00:54:56.160 --> 00:54:59.700
and operational performance,
they're all tied together.

00:54:59.700 --> 00:55:02.400
And what we're consistently saying

00:55:02.400 --> 00:55:04.279
to NHS colleagues is just keep going.

00:55:04.279 --> 00:55:05.670
You've all done plans,

00:55:05.670 --> 00:55:09.060
we've all got plans now
in aggregate, keep going.

00:55:09.060 --> 00:55:11.580
And Elizabeth I and I
will work with government

00:55:11.580 --> 00:55:13.467
and resolve that as soon as we can.

00:55:13.467 --> 00:55:16.020
And we've had some good
sessions with James,

00:55:16.020 --> 00:55:17.820
new Secretary of State
about that in recent weeks.

00:55:17.820 --> 00:55:20.193
So that'll hopefully get resolved soon.

00:55:21.180 --> 00:55:23.310
Second point I think for
all of us is a bit of a risk

00:55:23.310 --> 00:55:26.070
of distraction, especially
around the change process

00:55:26.070 --> 00:55:28.980
and the things that we've
talked about last year.

00:55:28.980 --> 00:55:31.530
You know, with ICBs as well
with the operating model.

00:55:31.530 --> 00:55:33.480
We've got a lot of
change going on out there

00:55:33.480 --> 00:55:35.220
with regional structures

00:55:35.220 --> 00:55:38.130
and ICBs settling themselves

00:55:38.130 --> 00:55:41.580
down properly in our own
distraction with the processes.

00:55:41.580 --> 00:55:43.650
So we need to make sure we avoid

00:55:43.650 --> 00:55:47.580
and manage that as best we can.

00:55:47.580 --> 00:55:50.610
And then there's a related
point which we talk about a lot,

00:55:50.610 --> 00:55:52.290
which is every now and again,

00:55:52.290 --> 00:55:56.460
the complexity in the fugdiness
builds itself back up.

00:55:56.460 --> 00:56:00.000
We've got to just keep
stripping this back every time.

00:56:00.000 --> 00:56:03.060
Core priorities, small number
of things, allow people

00:56:03.060 --> 00:56:05.190
to take manageable steps

00:56:05.190 --> 00:56:07.380
'cause we're trying to do an awful lot,

00:56:07.380 --> 00:56:09.210
everybody's trying to do an awful lot.

00:56:09.210 --> 00:56:13.170
So our ability to not get
caught up in the complexity

00:56:13.170 --> 00:56:15.093
is a big risk as well.

00:56:17.010 --> 00:56:17.843
- Okay.

00:56:17.843 --> 00:56:20.103
So I'm going to move us on to performance.

00:56:21.000 --> 00:56:23.730
Thank you for sharing all of the data.

00:56:23.730 --> 00:56:24.880
There's loads in there.

00:56:26.220 --> 00:56:28.833
I don't suggest, obviously
we talk through it,

00:56:30.420 --> 00:56:34.500
but I think the two pages, the areas

00:56:34.500 --> 00:56:36.570
where we've improved are great.

00:56:36.570 --> 00:56:37.650
Don't suggest we go through those,

00:56:37.650 --> 00:56:39.570
but we should absolutely
celebrate success there

00:56:39.570 --> 00:56:42.240
and make sure we capture
the learnings from those.

00:56:42.240 --> 00:56:46.710
But rather, can I suggest
that we come to the areas

00:56:46.710 --> 00:56:50.880
of challenge performance, those six areas.

00:56:50.880 --> 00:56:52.680
And in particular, I want to hear,

00:56:52.680 --> 00:56:57.423
given that many of these have
been there for a long time,

00:56:59.250 --> 00:57:02.070
I think we understand most
of them I haven't seen,

00:57:02.070 --> 00:57:03.630
but maybe we pick this
up at our strategy day,

00:57:03.630 --> 00:57:07.260
the detailed analysis
behind the diagnostic waits

00:57:07.260 --> 00:57:08.520
and what's going on there

00:57:08.520 --> 00:57:09.500
given that we know we've got quite a lot

00:57:09.500 --> 00:57:11.490
of capacity in quite a lot of places.

00:57:11.490 --> 00:57:13.710
But anyway, but on the other five,

00:57:13.710 --> 00:57:15.543
what are we very explicitly doing?

00:57:18.510 --> 00:57:19.830
- Thank you, Penny.

00:57:19.830 --> 00:57:21.390
As you said, I think it's really important

00:57:21.390 --> 00:57:23.820
to acknowledge the improvement performance

00:57:23.820 --> 00:57:25.650
that Jim has just mentioned.

00:57:25.650 --> 00:57:28.680
I will just state that my thanks

00:57:28.680 --> 00:57:31.860
to all the colleagues in the
NHS who've made it happen.

00:57:31.860 --> 00:57:35.763
So essentially, we delivered
more elective care in '25, '26

00:57:35.763 --> 00:57:37.560
than any point in its history,

00:57:37.560 --> 00:57:40.710
including providing 30 million tests,

00:57:40.710 --> 00:57:43.650
checks and scans, which
helped cut the waiting list

00:57:43.650 --> 00:57:48.650
by 312,000 patients
over the past 12 months

00:57:49.110 --> 00:57:54.110
with the waiting list
decreasing to 7,106,135,

00:57:54.360 --> 00:57:57.510
its lowest in 3 1/2 years.

00:57:57.510 --> 00:58:02.510
We also hit the 18-week plan of 65%.

00:58:03.000 --> 00:58:07.800
With 65.3%, were waiting
less than 18 weeks.

00:58:07.800 --> 00:58:11.450
We saw a record number
of patients in our A&amp;Es

00:58:11.450 --> 00:58:15.600
and our four-hour performance was 77.1%,

00:58:15.600 --> 00:58:20.520
which is a 2.1% increase
compared to March 25

00:58:20.520 --> 00:58:22.440
and the 12 hour A&amp;E performance,

00:58:22.440 --> 00:58:25.590
which is patients not
waiting longer than 12 hours,

00:58:25.590 --> 00:58:28.140
was 9% in March '26,

00:58:28.140 --> 00:58:32.640
which is also an improvement
from 9.9% the previous year.

00:58:32.640 --> 00:58:33.990
Ambulance response times

00:58:33.990 --> 00:58:38.340
for category two call outs
were more than five minutes

00:58:38.340 --> 00:58:40.590
faster last year than the year before.

00:58:40.590 --> 00:58:45.056
And we delivered more than
1.5 million appointments

00:58:45.056 --> 00:58:46.710
in general practise

00:58:46.710 --> 00:58:49.983
with satisfaction with
general practise rising.

00:58:52.200 --> 00:58:54.600
With all of this, there was
also productivity growth

00:58:54.600 --> 00:58:57.570
with which Elizabeth will pick up of 2.8%

00:58:57.570 --> 00:59:00.195
between January 26

00:59:00.195 --> 00:59:01.969
and April 25, which is excellent.

00:59:01.969 --> 00:59:04.770
And we are talking about productivity,

00:59:04.770 --> 00:59:07.350
we're talking also about
clinical operational bucket

00:59:07.350 --> 00:59:10.080
of productivity such
as length of stay, etc.

00:59:10.080 --> 00:59:11.880
or theatre utilisation.

00:59:11.880 --> 00:59:13.650
In terms of talking therapies,

00:59:13.650 --> 00:59:16.470
more than eight in 10 people referred

00:59:16.470 --> 00:59:19.740
to the NHS talking therapies
access the services

00:59:19.740 --> 00:59:21.420
within six weeks.

00:59:21.420 --> 00:59:24.450
And significant improvements really.

00:59:24.450 --> 00:59:27.300
And as you said, Penny, there
are some challenge areas,

00:59:27.300 --> 00:59:29.350
but there are a couple other improvements

00:59:30.772 --> 00:59:32.790
which are not in headlines,
which are adult in patients

00:59:32.790 --> 00:59:35.553
who are autistic or have
a learning disability,

00:59:36.720 --> 00:59:39.990
performance improving their
breast screening coverage,

00:59:39.990 --> 00:59:42.150
bowel screening coverage, hypertension

00:59:42.150 --> 00:59:44.580
and cholesterol management,
which then links us back

00:59:44.580 --> 00:59:47.970
to our modern service framework
for cardiovascular disease,

00:59:47.970 --> 00:59:51.120
access to preferred healthcare
professional crisis care,

00:59:51.120 --> 00:59:55.020
face-to-face contact within 24
hours and readmission rates.

00:59:55.020 --> 00:59:56.760
The challenges then, as we know

00:59:56.760 --> 01:00:00.570
and you've mentioned, are
the MMR vaccination rate,

01:00:00.570 --> 01:00:02.850
which stands at 83.8%,

01:00:02.850 --> 01:00:07.230
which is actually slightly
lower than the previous year.

01:00:07.230 --> 01:00:09.780
And it remains below WHO thresholds,

01:00:09.780 --> 01:00:13.020
posing continued risk of outbreaks.

01:00:13.020 --> 01:00:17.940
So, you know, there really is
joint action between ourselves

01:00:17.940 --> 01:00:21.030
and DHSC colleagues like Catherine Frances

01:00:21.030 --> 01:00:25.080
and the CMO Chris Whitty
working together not only

01:00:25.080 --> 01:00:28.080
to increase public awareness,
improve access, etc.

01:00:28.080 --> 01:00:31.500
but clearly, this has been
going on for many years

01:00:31.500 --> 01:00:36.500
and my take on this is it's going to have

01:00:36.930 --> 01:00:41.580
to be a constant reminder
for the population

01:00:41.580 --> 01:00:45.210
to how important vaccination
is, and not just MMR

01:00:45.210 --> 01:00:47.510
but all of the vaccinations
that are on offer.

01:00:48.660 --> 01:00:52.170
Referrals for continuing
healthcare within 28 days.

01:00:52.170 --> 01:00:56.523
Again, performance fell
to 74.2% from 77.6%.

01:00:58.950 --> 01:01:01.380
I'm afraid I don't have
the detail on this, Penny,

01:01:01.380 --> 01:01:04.173
but I can take this away
and we can discuss this.

01:01:05.760 --> 01:01:10.755
Staff sickness absence
was, again, slightly worse

01:01:10.755 --> 01:01:14.630
and I know that, Sir
Andrew Morris has requested

01:01:14.630 --> 01:01:17.725
a deep dive, which is
coming to the next F&amp;P,

01:01:17.725 --> 01:01:21.100
Finance and Performance
Committee in a couple of months

01:01:21.958 --> 01:01:25.230
on all staff engagement,
staff sickness measures.

01:01:25.230 --> 01:01:26.490
Okay, I'll stop at the point.

01:01:26.490 --> 01:01:28.770
- Well, can I ask, so
healthy life expectancy?

01:01:28.770 --> 01:01:29.910
- Oh yes.

01:01:29.910 --> 01:01:31.800
There is a detailed annex

01:01:31.800 --> 01:01:36.390
to this report following on
from the last board meeting

01:01:36.390 --> 01:01:41.227
on healthy life expectancy,
which we know is 60, 61 years.

01:01:42.780 --> 01:01:45.870
And what the team have
done is given an analysis

01:01:45.870 --> 01:01:49.470
based on region and
deprivation in the annex.

01:01:49.470 --> 01:01:54.390
So we can actually see
exactly where and how.

01:01:55.230 --> 01:01:57.750
And it relates to the actions.

01:01:57.750 --> 01:01:59.040
Yeah, it relates to the actions.

01:01:59.040 --> 01:02:00.900
- So if we want to address that,

01:02:00.900 --> 01:02:01.860
we know we need

01:02:01.860 --> 01:02:04.170
a fundamentally different
model of healthcare.

01:02:04.170 --> 01:02:06.300
We know we need to move
where the money goes,

01:02:06.300 --> 01:02:07.260
we know we need to change

01:02:07.260 --> 01:02:09.210
where the workforce goes and so on.

01:02:09.210 --> 01:02:10.230
So I think, you know,

01:02:10.230 --> 01:02:11.690
Paul will say we're going
to pick a quite a lot

01:02:11.690 --> 01:02:13.530
of this up at our strategy day in July,

01:02:13.530 --> 01:02:15.930
but that's a bit, I just want to make sure

01:02:15.930 --> 01:02:18.090
it's fully logged in
a public board meeting

01:02:18.090 --> 01:02:20.400
that we recognise criticality of this

01:02:20.400 --> 01:02:21.760
for all of our strategy

01:02:23.760 --> 01:02:25.620
as well as looking at
where the challenges are.

01:02:25.620 --> 01:02:27.270
But we've sort of known
that for a long time.

01:02:27.270 --> 01:02:30.420
And then on the discharge
delays, which again,

01:02:30.420 --> 01:02:33.063
very long standing issue and challenge.

01:02:34.650 --> 01:02:36.755
- Sarah-Jane, I don't know if
you want to comment on that.

01:02:36.755 --> 01:02:40.890
- Yeah, I mean, it's a deterioration

01:02:40.890 --> 01:02:44.940
across all the different
reasons for discharge.

01:02:44.940 --> 01:02:47.790
So we've been doing a lot of work with,

01:02:47.790 --> 01:02:49.680
as we alluded to earlier, with chief exec,

01:02:49.680 --> 01:02:50.790
chief operating officers

01:02:50.790 --> 01:02:54.510
and others, but also
colleagues from social care

01:02:54.510 --> 01:02:58.800
on what steps of what we've described

01:02:58.800 --> 01:03:00.210
as the model discharge pathway.

01:03:00.210 --> 01:03:02.010
And we are due to share that

01:03:02.010 --> 01:03:03.300
with the service in the next few weeks.

01:03:03.300 --> 01:03:05.730
But all we've really done is source

01:03:05.730 --> 01:03:07.530
the best practise from out there.

01:03:07.530 --> 01:03:09.000
But it's exactly as Meghana said,

01:03:09.000 --> 01:03:10.380
it's not a single reason.

01:03:10.380 --> 01:03:14.010
It's that relentless,
you know, focus each day

01:03:14.010 --> 01:03:17.550
and ensuring that people
leave hospital in the morning

01:03:17.550 --> 01:03:19.740
in line with the admissions
as they come in and so on.

01:03:19.740 --> 01:03:24.150
So yeah, there's not a big
strategic answer, Penny.

01:03:24.150 --> 01:03:25.800
It's down in the operational detail

01:03:25.800 --> 01:03:28.050
of just delivering those
processes day in day out,

01:03:28.050 --> 01:03:30.900
which that model discharge
document will help

01:03:30.900 --> 01:03:33.510
I think people on the front line to use.

01:03:33.510 --> 01:03:35.730
- So I was very struck by a trust

01:03:35.730 --> 01:03:37.200
that is struggling financially,

01:03:37.200 --> 01:03:38.900
who Elizabeth and I have met with.

01:03:40.410 --> 01:03:42.300
If they could address some of that,

01:03:42.300 --> 01:03:44.970
that's three wards that they could close.

01:03:44.970 --> 01:03:46.220
And that's a small trust.

01:03:47.070 --> 01:03:49.410
So I think we've got to
keep coming back to it

01:03:49.410 --> 01:03:52.500
and it fits in with what
is the long term plan

01:03:52.500 --> 01:03:53.433
to address this.

01:03:56.010 --> 01:03:58.160
Things colleagues would
like to pick up on?

01:04:00.540 --> 01:04:01.860
Okay, I've just got one
final question then,

01:04:01.860 --> 01:04:04.200
which is on the data
that we don't have yet,

01:04:04.200 --> 01:04:05.550
some of which absolutely critical

01:04:05.550 --> 01:04:07.080
as we again, focusing much more

01:04:07.080 --> 01:04:10.590
on primary care, community care and so on,

01:04:10.590 --> 01:04:12.030
when will we get that data?

01:04:12.030 --> 01:04:15.630
So in particular, the occupied bed days

01:04:15.630 --> 01:04:17.643
for 100,000 or acute bed days.

01:04:21.120 --> 01:04:25.258
It just says that it's not
yet ready, but is that?

01:04:25.258 --> 01:04:27.060
- Chair, the next thing

01:04:27.060 --> 01:04:29.850
that the team are focusing on is the new

01:04:29.850 --> 01:04:31.140
national oversight framework

01:04:31.140 --> 01:04:34.110
and aligning all the
metrics and the IPR to that

01:04:34.110 --> 01:04:36.340
and also, as you mentioned, the quality

01:04:37.260 --> 01:04:40.320
national quality board
dataset, the compendium.

01:04:40.320 --> 01:04:43.500
I will check if that
particular metric is in it.

01:04:43.500 --> 01:04:45.150
- Yeah, because I think we've got it.

01:04:45.150 --> 01:04:47.940
- I was going to say, so
I think we've got it in,

01:04:47.940 --> 01:04:50.100
I don't know why it's not
found it way into the report.

01:04:50.100 --> 01:04:50.980
So I'm happy to work with Meghana

01:04:50.980 --> 01:04:52.710
to ensure that to happen

01:04:52.710 --> 01:04:56.073
'cause I'm definitely
using it different ways,

01:04:57.526 --> 01:04:59.130
so I can help with that.

01:04:59.130 --> 01:05:00.360
- Okay, good.

01:05:00.360 --> 01:05:02.289
So up to you Elizabeth for the moment.

01:05:02.289 --> 01:05:03.240
- Yeah, thank you.

01:05:03.240 --> 01:05:05.793
So it actually gives me some pleasure to,

01:05:06.810 --> 01:05:08.880
we've been talking about
this for a little while

01:05:08.880 --> 01:05:11.880
and this just to say this
report doesn't really do justice

01:05:11.880 --> 01:05:13.050
to the achievements,

01:05:13.050 --> 01:05:17.850
especially when you also
think about the achievements

01:05:17.850 --> 01:05:19.860
that Meghana has just
described on performance.

01:05:19.860 --> 01:05:22.200
So this isn't just,
we've achieved the money,

01:05:22.200 --> 01:05:23.790
we've achieved the money while it's making

01:05:23.790 --> 01:05:26.400
significant improvements in performance.

01:05:26.400 --> 01:05:28.830
And obviously, we've
seen those that coming

01:05:28.830 --> 01:05:30.450
through in outcomes as well.

01:05:30.450 --> 01:05:33.480
But if I just briefly
summarise from the report

01:05:33.480 --> 01:05:34.860
that you have in front of you.

01:05:34.860 --> 01:05:36.090
So final report,

01:05:36.090 --> 01:05:37.410
we've been talking about
this a little while,

01:05:37.410 --> 01:05:39.210
saying we think we are going to achieve,

01:05:39.210 --> 01:05:41.550
I'm pleased to say it's unaudited,

01:05:41.550 --> 01:05:45.570
but as of the end of March 25, 26,

01:05:45.570 --> 01:05:49.260
the overall financial position
is that the NHS is in balance

01:05:49.260 --> 01:05:52.230
delivering a 70 million pound underspend,

01:05:52.230 --> 01:05:57.090
which is like no 0.04%
at a national level.

01:05:57.090 --> 01:05:58.260
Really importantly,

01:05:58.260 --> 01:06:01.170
and Jim's described this
previously, we've achieved that

01:06:01.170 --> 01:06:03.540
without recourse of going
back to the treasury

01:06:03.540 --> 01:06:05.400
for the first time in a decade.

01:06:05.400 --> 01:06:08.370
And this is a significant
step for the sector

01:06:08.370 --> 01:06:10.710
for everyone actually to show

01:06:10.710 --> 01:06:12.780
that the NHS can manage this money

01:06:12.780 --> 01:06:14.280
has got financial discipline

01:06:14.280 --> 01:06:17.910
and reinforces our
credibility with government.

01:06:17.910 --> 01:06:20.010
Yesterday, there was a
cross government meeting

01:06:20.010 --> 01:06:25.010
and the treasury noted this
and we've now got the benefit

01:06:28.890 --> 01:06:33.175
of actually a productivity
meeting across government to show

01:06:33.175 --> 01:06:35.323
how we've done that

01:06:35.323 --> 01:06:36.780
and how we've achieved this in aggregate.

01:06:36.780 --> 01:06:38.790
So there's something about that,

01:06:38.790 --> 01:06:40.740
but it is a significant step,

01:06:40.740 --> 01:06:45.090
especially when you think in
April last year we were facing

01:06:45.090 --> 01:06:50.090
a 4.4 billion pound deficit
and that's the net position

01:06:50.280 --> 01:06:53.250
after deficit support funding.

01:06:53.250 --> 01:06:57.270
Following the reset in March
in a very short period of time,

01:06:57.270 --> 01:06:59.230
actually, we got to a balanced plan

01:07:00.150 --> 01:07:02.610
at the start of the year,
again, just re-rehearing

01:07:02.610 --> 01:07:05.193
'cause I think it's
important to tell the story.

01:07:06.150 --> 01:07:08.880
Lots of people, including
some people out in the service

01:07:08.880 --> 01:07:11.310
thought that this was un-deliverable.

01:07:11.310 --> 01:07:12.630
But the camaraderie and the way

01:07:12.630 --> 01:07:15.030
that people have worked
together has been exceptional,

01:07:15.030 --> 01:07:16.140
I would say, so.

01:07:16.140 --> 01:07:20.280
And then against this,
we've talked about it today.

01:07:20.280 --> 01:07:22.650
So last year, we had industrial action.

01:07:22.650 --> 01:07:25.620
We had a significant amount
of organisational change,

01:07:25.620 --> 01:07:28.743
particularly in our ICBs,
so the 50% headcount.

01:07:29.816 --> 01:07:31.350
And we went on

01:07:31.350 --> 01:07:34.740
to have a very interesting
last quarter of the year

01:07:34.740 --> 01:07:36.570
around our elective
recovery and the sprint.

01:07:36.570 --> 01:07:39.240
So really, really pleased to say

01:07:39.240 --> 01:07:41.850
that we've balanced the position.

01:07:41.850 --> 01:07:43.886
When you look at the
report, you can see however,

01:07:43.886 --> 01:07:47.100
in aggregate we have got
a number of providers

01:07:47.100 --> 01:07:49.383
and systems that were in deficit,

01:07:50.730 --> 01:07:54.240
but I can honestly say hand
on heart other than 1 or 2

01:07:54.240 --> 01:07:55.740
that I'm not going to mention.

01:07:55.740 --> 01:07:59.100
The vast, vast majority,
even after month nine

01:07:59.100 --> 01:08:00.630
when it became a bit bumpy,

01:08:00.630 --> 01:08:04.260
continue to push to the end of March

01:08:04.260 --> 01:08:05.310
and it would be easier.

01:08:05.310 --> 01:08:07.690
Historically, we've seen actually

01:08:08.640 --> 01:08:12.000
financial position has
gone off in month nine

01:08:12.000 --> 01:08:13.980
and then we've not recovered it.

01:08:13.980 --> 01:08:15.450
That's not what's happened this year.

01:08:15.450 --> 01:08:17.010
So that's really testament

01:08:17.010 --> 01:08:18.450
'cause it would be easier to stand back

01:08:18.450 --> 01:08:21.300
and move that problem into
the following financial year.

01:08:21.300 --> 01:08:22.560
So that's great.

01:08:22.560 --> 01:08:26.670
And the percentage of organisations
delivering their plans

01:08:26.670 --> 01:08:28.650
increased this year to 80%.

01:08:28.650 --> 01:08:33.090
So efficiency delivered
at 10.2 billion pounds.

01:08:33.090 --> 01:08:37.230
Agency almost halved
from 2.1 billion to 1.2

01:08:37.230 --> 01:08:40.890
and we've got plans to reduce
that significantly further.

01:08:40.890 --> 01:08:45.030
Examples where we had providers
that had agency budgets

01:08:45.030 --> 01:08:49.710
of up to 12 million a year
last year that was reduced

01:08:49.710 --> 01:08:51.330
to 400,000 per annum.

01:08:51.330 --> 01:08:53.550
So some stunning, stunning performance

01:08:53.550 --> 01:08:55.920
that we are making sure we are sharing

01:08:55.920 --> 01:08:58.320
that information across the CFO community.

01:08:58.320 --> 01:08:59.310
And actually, we did it through

01:08:59.310 --> 01:09:00.750
our regional road shows as well.

01:09:00.750 --> 01:09:03.090
So really understanding, you know,

01:09:03.090 --> 01:09:05.310
what's driven this change.

01:09:05.310 --> 01:09:08.040
And then on capital, again, you know,

01:09:08.040 --> 01:09:11.070
a real scarce resource
is, I talk to people,

01:09:11.070 --> 01:09:13.650
particularly chief
executives about capital

01:09:13.650 --> 01:09:15.360
as much as I talk to people about revenue

01:09:15.360 --> 01:09:17.490
because if you want to drive productivity,

01:09:17.490 --> 01:09:21.390
you've got to get fit-for-purpose estate.

01:09:21.390 --> 01:09:23.250
Currently, we have unfortunately

01:09:23.250 --> 01:09:25.890
where we've had a shortfall in
capital, we've replaced that

01:09:25.890 --> 01:09:29.400
with headcount and manual change.

01:09:29.400 --> 01:09:32.220
So we're trying to address that.

01:09:32.220 --> 01:09:35.790
So of the 9.1 billion

01:09:35.790 --> 01:09:40.790
of capital out in the service,
I think 98% of it was spent.

01:09:40.890 --> 01:09:42.330
There was a small underspend,

01:09:42.330 --> 01:09:44.250
doesn't sound small when
I read the number out,

01:09:44.250 --> 01:09:48.930
but it's 321 million of that
200 million of it was planned

01:09:48.930 --> 01:09:50.190
'cause we were working across the group.

01:09:50.190 --> 01:09:53.880
So the actual underspend was 120 million.

01:09:53.880 --> 01:09:57.270
And I would just go back
to a period when I was CFO

01:09:57.270 --> 01:09:59.973
many years ago, that number
was actually a billion.

01:10:00.840 --> 01:10:02.730
That's the amount we used
to underspend each year.

01:10:02.730 --> 01:10:05.460
Can see David nodding, you
can remember can't you?

01:10:05.460 --> 01:10:06.900
So to get it down from a billion

01:10:06.900 --> 01:10:09.000
to 120 million is something.

01:10:09.000 --> 01:10:11.940
And we did that through a revised process

01:10:11.940 --> 01:10:13.170
starting at quarter three.

01:10:13.170 --> 01:10:17.580
So again, the service really
worked through with us.

01:10:17.580 --> 01:10:20.280
And then just thinking about
Ravi's question, if I may,

01:10:20.280 --> 01:10:22.980
and something you posed to Jim.

01:10:22.980 --> 01:10:26.730
I think that the change this
year has been absolutely

01:10:26.730 --> 01:10:31.230
the strong leadership
ownership, transparency of data,

01:10:31.230 --> 01:10:33.840
Clarity about the rules

01:10:33.840 --> 01:10:36.180
and this real sense of we
are all in it together.

01:10:36.180 --> 01:10:38.160
It's been remarkable actually.

01:10:38.160 --> 01:10:40.080
The amount of sharing that's gone on

01:10:40.080 --> 01:10:43.290
and actually trying to
really think through

01:10:43.290 --> 01:10:44.820
not only the art of the possible,

01:10:44.820 --> 01:10:46.350
but the sharing of resources

01:10:46.350 --> 01:10:47.790
and thinking very differently

01:10:47.790 --> 01:10:50.193
has been quite a profound
change in my view.

01:10:51.150 --> 01:10:55.140
So just to say in closing,
I just wanted to thank

01:10:55.140 --> 01:10:58.860
particularly, so finance is
definitely the responsibility

01:10:58.860 --> 01:11:01.770
of the board, but I would like
to personally go on record

01:11:01.770 --> 01:11:05.100
and thank the finance
and the CFO community,

01:11:05.100 --> 01:11:06.720
not just for what's been achieved

01:11:06.720 --> 01:11:08.250
but also for the camaraderie

01:11:08.250 --> 01:11:09.750
and the support to me personally.

01:11:09.750 --> 01:11:10.850
So thank you for that.

01:11:12.090 --> 01:11:15.360
- So I've got David then Frankie,

01:11:15.360 --> 01:11:19.260
- Well first of all, to
echo the achievement here,

01:11:19.260 --> 01:11:21.630
I remember one of your
predecessors a long while ago

01:11:21.630 --> 01:11:22.980
saying to me, this is like trying

01:11:22.980 --> 01:11:25.203
to land a jumbo jet on a postage stamp.

01:11:26.160 --> 01:11:27.630
You don't want to come in with a deficit,

01:11:27.630 --> 01:11:29.610
but you don't want to come in
with a large surplus either.

01:11:29.610 --> 01:11:31.860
It's really an achievement.

01:11:31.860 --> 01:11:33.630
I've got this stupid question though.

01:11:33.630 --> 01:11:37.380
When we're looking at tables
like table two with systems

01:11:37.380 --> 01:11:41.073
with deficits, if you
want to ask the question,

01:11:42.510 --> 01:11:45.510
how well have they done,
again, each system,

01:11:45.510 --> 01:11:49.980
each provider against the
target they've been set,

01:11:49.980 --> 01:11:51.963
which number should we be looking at?

01:11:53.760 --> 01:11:57.180
Including deficit support funding

01:11:57.180 --> 01:12:00.270
or excluding deficit support funding?

01:12:00.270 --> 01:12:01.860
- It's a really good question.

01:12:01.860 --> 01:12:04.243
It's a tricky one because
of the rules that,

01:12:04.243 --> 01:12:06.393
and I know why you're asking by the way,

01:12:07.346 --> 01:12:11.008
so it's a tricky one because clearly,

01:12:11.008 --> 01:12:14.250
we agreed in this rules-based system

01:12:14.250 --> 01:12:18.600
that there would be an element
of deficit support funding

01:12:18.600 --> 01:12:20.460
'cause it'd been there historically.

01:12:20.460 --> 01:12:24.360
We would be looking to unwind
that deficit support funding

01:12:24.360 --> 01:12:25.560
over the next three years

01:12:25.560 --> 01:12:27.510
and putting it into baseline allocations

01:12:27.510 --> 01:12:29.310
'cause it shouldn't be there really.

01:12:30.150 --> 01:12:33.060
But in doing that, what
we said was at the start

01:12:33.060 --> 01:12:36.513
of the year, you can earn it.

01:12:37.860 --> 01:12:39.900
Yeah, so you can earn it
because you've submitted

01:12:39.900 --> 01:12:44.310
a board approved plan and this
is a incentive I would say.

01:12:44.310 --> 01:12:46.170
You've submitted a board approved plan

01:12:46.170 --> 01:12:48.000
and if you deliver your
plan, you'll earn it.

01:12:48.000 --> 01:12:49.680
If you don't deliver your plan,

01:12:49.680 --> 01:12:51.510
you won't get a full access to it.

01:12:51.510 --> 01:12:55.980
Now, people will challenge that
around, are you penalising?

01:12:55.980 --> 01:12:59.160
But actually we are all
locking arms as an NHS

01:12:59.160 --> 01:13:01.020
and we were saying we are going to try

01:13:01.020 --> 01:13:02.190
and deliver our money this year.

01:13:02.190 --> 01:13:07.020
So I would say that the
answer at a local level,

01:13:07.020 --> 01:13:10.200
people will absolutely
report to the boards.

01:13:10.200 --> 01:13:12.060
If I'd received the
deficit support funding,

01:13:12.060 --> 01:13:13.950
this would've been my net position.

01:13:13.950 --> 01:13:15.990
But actually, this is my reported position

01:13:15.990 --> 01:13:17.430
'cause I didn't receive that.

01:13:17.430 --> 01:13:18.570
And what we did as well, David,

01:13:18.570 --> 01:13:20.635
was the deficit support funding.

01:13:20.635 --> 01:13:23.760
We recycled it back in to those.

01:13:23.760 --> 01:13:26.610
- So simple terms, which is it including

01:13:26.610 --> 01:13:29.070
or excluding, which is the
number that's telling me

01:13:29.070 --> 01:13:32.223
how well they're doing against
what they should be doing?

01:13:33.810 --> 01:13:34.643
- So I-

01:13:34.643 --> 01:13:36.378
- Excluding.
- It's excluding, yeah.

01:13:36.378 --> 01:13:37.470
- Okay.
- Yeah, sorry.

01:13:37.470 --> 01:13:38.820
- Thank you.

01:13:38.820 --> 01:13:41.220
- However, and that will
come to Frankie though,

01:13:43.680 --> 01:13:47.520
over a year ago and you and
I joined the board meeting,

01:13:47.520 --> 01:13:49.560
the previous team,

01:13:49.560 --> 01:13:52.380
there was a conversation
about allocations.

01:13:52.380 --> 01:13:55.895
And I think we still haven't
quite bottomed out, you know,

01:13:55.895 --> 01:13:59.970
are the allocations being allocated

01:13:59.970 --> 01:14:01.860
in the fairest way

01:14:01.860 --> 01:14:03.660
and it's always going
to be really difficult.

01:14:03.660 --> 01:14:04.815
But yeah, we'll come
back outside in a minute.

01:14:04.815 --> 01:14:06.480
I'll take some of the
other questions as well.

01:14:06.480 --> 01:14:09.210
So Frankie, do you want
to go and then Glen.

01:14:09.210 --> 01:14:10.160
- Thank you, Penny.

01:14:11.310 --> 01:14:13.290
The discussion has really made me reflect

01:14:13.290 --> 01:14:14.460
on Glen's earlier point.

01:14:14.460 --> 01:14:16.620
I think what's really made
the difference this year

01:14:16.620 --> 01:14:21.030
is that absolute clarity and
focus in the central leadership

01:14:21.030 --> 01:14:23.550
and how that has been translated
throughout the service

01:14:23.550 --> 01:14:27.600
and with that clarity that
the rules are the rules

01:14:27.600 --> 01:14:29.640
that's allowed every member of staff

01:14:29.640 --> 01:14:32.070
to really get behind it and buy in.

01:14:32.070 --> 01:14:34.350
And then the success breeds success.

01:14:34.350 --> 01:14:37.470
Just as Glen was saying earlier,
in operationally, you know,

01:14:37.470 --> 01:14:40.620
when you see corridor
care being eliminated,

01:14:40.620 --> 01:14:43.860
when you see your flow improving,
you get a sense of pride

01:14:43.860 --> 01:14:48.030
and joy and when you deliver
financially, it engenders

01:14:48.030 --> 01:14:49.470
that similar positivity.

01:14:49.470 --> 01:14:52.140
And I think that's really,
really vital for the engagement

01:14:52.140 --> 01:14:53.820
of our staff because it's them

01:14:53.820 --> 01:14:55.500
who are allowing us to deliver this.

01:14:55.500 --> 01:14:56.430
- Yeah, agreed.

01:14:56.430 --> 01:14:57.263
Glen.

01:14:57.263 --> 01:14:59.710
- I was just going to park
answer David's question

01:15:01.050 --> 01:15:03.480
obviously, in the oversight framework

01:15:03.480 --> 01:15:04.980
we have the financial override.

01:15:04.980 --> 01:15:07.890
So an organisation that is in deficit

01:15:07.890 --> 01:15:11.280
or in receipt of deficit
support funding cannot get

01:15:11.280 --> 01:15:12.630
above a segment three.

01:15:12.630 --> 01:15:15.450
So actually, over the course of this year,

01:15:15.450 --> 01:15:18.540
that has changed people's
approach is to having

01:15:18.540 --> 01:15:20.040
what may have been seen in the past

01:15:20.040 --> 01:15:21.626
as an acceptable deficit
into organisations

01:15:21.626 --> 01:15:23.280
trying to drive that down.

01:15:23.280 --> 01:15:24.113
- Definitely.

01:15:24.113 --> 01:15:26.790
- So that I think would
reinforce them the excluding too.

01:15:27.630 --> 01:15:28.463
- On allocation.

01:15:28.463 --> 01:15:31.158
- Yeah, on allocation so
I don't have 200 emails

01:15:31.158 --> 01:15:34.050
of why the allocation formula is wrong.

01:15:34.050 --> 01:15:36.000
I'm really pleased to say
that we've obviously got

01:15:36.000 --> 01:15:40.290
an independent body that looks
at the allocation formula.

01:15:40.290 --> 01:15:43.830
We have asked them to
particularly look at a review

01:15:43.830 --> 01:15:45.420
of deprivation.

01:15:45.420 --> 01:15:46.680
And we've also asked them

01:15:46.680 --> 01:15:49.703
to look at some of the Chris
Whitty reports into aging well

01:15:49.703 --> 01:15:51.870
and rural and coastal communities.

01:15:51.870 --> 01:15:54.790
So I'm pleased to say
that it is independent

01:15:55.840 --> 01:15:57.420
and we will wait 'cause
that those reviews,

01:15:57.420 --> 01:15:59.610
that review work has started.

01:15:59.610 --> 01:16:01.410
- When does it report?

01:16:01.410 --> 01:16:03.090
- I don't think it's
for a 12-month actually,

01:16:03.090 --> 01:16:04.740
but can I go back and check?

01:16:04.740 --> 01:16:06.300
- [Penny] It does take quite a, yeah.

01:16:06.300 --> 01:16:07.350
- It does to do it well.

01:16:07.350 --> 01:16:11.730
And they have reconstituted, you know,

01:16:11.730 --> 01:16:14.280
the independent mechanisms as well.

01:16:14.280 --> 01:16:16.050
So it does.

01:16:16.050 --> 01:16:17.010
And then, sorry, Penny.

01:16:17.010 --> 01:16:18.933
The only other thing I would say is,

01:16:20.100 --> 01:16:23.853
'cause Jim will probably point me to this,

01:16:24.840 --> 01:16:28.470
clearly, we've just had one
month, one results coming in.

01:16:28.470 --> 01:16:29.880
II couldn't bring them to the board

01:16:29.880 --> 01:16:31.440
'cause they've just come in

01:16:31.440 --> 01:16:33.060
but I would give a verbal update.

01:16:33.060 --> 01:16:36.690
So we are on forecast, no change.

01:16:36.690 --> 01:16:38.610
We still have a planned deficit

01:16:38.610 --> 01:16:40.260
and we'll be talking about planning

01:16:40.260 --> 01:16:42.060
in the private board later

01:16:42.060 --> 01:16:44.820
and that planned deficit
number is reducing.

01:16:44.820 --> 01:16:46.260
So we've had a number of board to boards

01:16:46.260 --> 01:16:49.083
and people continue to
work on those plans.

01:16:50.310 --> 01:16:54.930
But as of month one year to
date, we're off 150 million.

01:16:54.930 --> 01:16:57.300
A lot of people are pointing to the cost

01:16:57.300 --> 01:17:00.690
of industrial action at
the start of the year.

01:17:00.690 --> 01:17:02.700
But clearly, I'm really pleased to say

01:17:02.700 --> 01:17:04.560
that people are saying they
think they're going to be back

01:17:04.560 --> 01:17:06.690
on track by the end of the quarter.

01:17:06.690 --> 01:17:09.780
So that's what we've
been told consistently.

01:17:09.780 --> 01:17:12.720
But that's the topic I think that everyone

01:17:12.720 --> 01:17:14.070
is now focused on.

01:17:14.070 --> 01:17:14.903
- Very good.

01:17:14.903 --> 01:17:15.870
So I think how well we
could have done without.

01:17:15.870 --> 01:17:17.040
- Industrial action.

01:17:17.040 --> 01:17:19.323
- I know, I know, that's true.

01:17:20.400 --> 01:17:22.320
- Well, how much more
we could have reinvested

01:17:22.320 --> 01:17:24.150
in improving health, I should say.

01:17:24.150 --> 01:17:25.950
- Yeah.
- Okay.

01:17:25.950 --> 01:17:30.950
So next item is on the
equality duties review report,

01:17:32.100 --> 01:17:33.813
which we need to approve.

01:17:35.550 --> 01:17:37.500
Shall I just ask colleagues
whether they're happy

01:17:37.500 --> 01:17:38.460
to approve it or?

01:17:38.460 --> 01:17:40.082
- [Speaker] Yeah, that's fine.

01:17:40.082 --> 01:17:41.100
- I thought it was very thorough report,

01:17:41.100 --> 01:17:42.200
so thank you for that.

01:17:43.230 --> 01:17:44.610
Colleagues okay with that?

01:17:44.610 --> 01:17:46.890
Okay, thank you both for doing that

01:17:46.890 --> 01:17:51.030
and very helpful to see.

01:17:51.030 --> 01:17:53.223
Okay, so then the next one, which,

01:17:54.210 --> 01:17:56.700
I don't think you need approval
for this, is that right?

01:17:56.700 --> 01:17:59.400
But we just need to be
aware that we do have

01:17:59.400 --> 01:18:03.870
these directions and mandatory requests.

01:18:03.870 --> 01:18:05.790
Is there anything else
that we need to know

01:18:05.790 --> 01:18:09.480
or can we take it that
you are acting on these?

01:18:09.480 --> 01:18:11.115
- I'm acting on these

01:18:11.115 --> 01:18:12.810
and they're just there for information.

01:18:12.810 --> 01:18:15.663
- Okay, does anyone have
any comments or questions?

01:18:16.800 --> 01:18:17.633
Okay, great.

01:18:17.633 --> 01:18:19.230
Well thank you for doing that.

01:18:19.230 --> 01:18:21.570
Right, so I'm going to
bring us on now to a number

01:18:21.570 --> 01:18:24.690
of questions that we've had
from members of the public.

01:18:24.690 --> 01:18:28.230
So what I will do is read out the question

01:18:28.230 --> 01:18:30.501
so that everyone is aware of what it is.

01:18:30.501 --> 01:18:33.810
And then the first one is for Glen

01:18:33.810 --> 01:18:35.670
and then there are a few,
I'm afraid for Frankie,

01:18:35.670 --> 01:18:37.830
bit of a baptism of fire for you.

01:18:37.830 --> 01:18:41.287
Everyone always has questions
for the medical director.

01:18:41.287 --> 01:18:44.190
So Glen, what progress has
been made in establishing

01:18:44.190 --> 01:18:48.870
a dedicated support for individuals
who seek to detransition

01:18:48.870 --> 01:18:51.300
following treatment for
gender-related care?

01:18:51.300 --> 01:18:53.820
Given it's now been two
years since the CAS review

01:18:53.820 --> 01:18:57.510
recommended one and the
consultation has been completed.

01:18:57.510 --> 01:19:00.120
In particular, how is NHS England ensuring

01:19:00.120 --> 01:19:02.340
that patients experiencing physical

01:19:02.340 --> 01:19:03.900
or mental health complications

01:19:03.900 --> 01:19:06.150
or needing specialist support are able

01:19:06.150 --> 01:19:10.110
to access appropriate services
and clear referral pathways?

01:19:10.110 --> 01:19:10.943
- Thank you, Chair.

01:19:10.943 --> 01:19:13.810
So an update from our
specialist commissioning team

01:19:14.778 --> 01:19:17.760
on children's and young
people's gender services.

01:19:17.760 --> 01:19:20.760
So we have now a new service spec

01:19:20.760 --> 01:19:23.370
that is based on the CAS review model.

01:19:23.370 --> 01:19:27.270
We actually have three services
that are up and running

01:19:27.270 --> 01:19:30.090
at Great Ormond Street, Bristol Alder Hey.

01:19:30.090 --> 01:19:32.850
We have two more going live in the autumn,

01:19:32.850 --> 01:19:34.890
Cambridge and Sheffield.

01:19:34.890 --> 01:19:36.780
And we have two more
scheduled for next year,

01:19:36.780 --> 01:19:38.790
which are Newcastle and Birmingham.

01:19:38.790 --> 01:19:40.530
So you can see we'll have a spread

01:19:40.530 --> 01:19:42.780
across the country at that stage.

01:19:42.780 --> 01:19:44.940
There's a national
provider network in place

01:19:44.940 --> 01:19:47.820
which has a research and oversight board.

01:19:47.820 --> 01:19:49.440
We've commissioned a new training

01:19:49.440 --> 01:19:51.870
and education programme for clinicians.

01:19:51.870 --> 01:19:55.830
We're out to public consultation
on a new access policy

01:19:55.830 --> 01:19:57.990
and we are currently
looking at the evidence

01:19:57.990 --> 01:20:00.480
around developing a
potential clinical pathway

01:20:00.480 --> 01:20:02.130
for detransition.

01:20:02.130 --> 01:20:04.110
So there's quite a lot of
work going on in that area.

01:20:04.110 --> 01:20:07.830
- And all of those sites offer
support for detransitioning?

01:20:07.830 --> 01:20:09.060
- They offer support but we are looking

01:20:09.060 --> 01:20:11.010
at the evidence on the pathway.

01:20:11.010 --> 01:20:12.100
- Yes, no I understand.

01:20:12.100 --> 01:20:13.830
Okay, all right, thank you.

01:20:13.830 --> 01:20:16.140
So Frankie, I'll do each one.

01:20:16.140 --> 01:20:21.090
So the first one is given
NHS England's Jess's Rule,

01:20:21.090 --> 01:20:24.210
which is about asking
GPs to reflect, review,

01:20:24.210 --> 01:20:26.970
and rethink if a patient presents
three times with the same

01:20:26.970 --> 01:20:28.480
or escalating symptoms

01:20:29.940 --> 01:20:33.000
and to consider where it's
appropriate to refer onwards

01:20:33.000 --> 01:20:36.240
for specialist input
in those circumstances.

01:20:36.240 --> 01:20:40.680
Given the single point of
access work, a consultant

01:20:40.680 --> 01:20:43.410
or non-consultant member of
the team will remotely review

01:20:43.410 --> 01:20:45.900
that referral and can then convert it

01:20:45.900 --> 01:20:47.403
into advice and guidance.

01:20:48.750 --> 01:20:51.660
Can the board confirm
whether these policies

01:20:51.660 --> 01:20:53.850
have been assessed for consistency

01:20:53.850 --> 01:20:56.850
and given these what happens to patients?

01:20:56.850 --> 01:20:58.350
- Thank you for the question.

01:20:58.350 --> 01:21:00.360
So the short answer is yes.

01:21:00.360 --> 01:21:02.580
Jess's Rule and the single
point of access system

01:21:02.580 --> 01:21:04.770
have both been assessed to ensure

01:21:04.770 --> 01:21:07.200
that they are consistent with one another.

01:21:07.200 --> 01:21:09.660
But to give a little more
detail, under Jess's rule,

01:21:09.660 --> 01:21:13.410
if a patient presents three
times with an unexpected

01:21:13.410 --> 01:21:18.240
or unexplained symptom,
Jess's rule asks the GP team

01:21:18.240 --> 01:21:21.543
to reflect, review, and rethink.

01:21:22.410 --> 01:21:24.030
So in terms of reflecting,

01:21:24.030 --> 01:21:26.640
the GP team will be asked to think back

01:21:26.640 --> 01:21:28.202
what has the patient said?

01:21:28.202 --> 01:21:30.150
What could have been missed?

01:21:30.150 --> 01:21:33.480
And offer ongoing continuity
so the patient can come back

01:21:33.480 --> 01:21:35.103
with the same practitioner.

01:21:36.000 --> 01:21:38.760
And if crucially, the
previous consultations

01:21:38.760 --> 01:21:41.430
have been virtual, bring
them back face-to-face.

01:21:41.430 --> 01:21:42.690
So that's reflect.

01:21:42.690 --> 01:21:43.950
In terms of review,

01:21:43.950 --> 01:21:47.430
that's about seeking a second
opinion, talking to peers

01:21:47.430 --> 01:21:48.780
and GP colleagues.

01:21:48.780 --> 01:21:50.280
Could there be something
else that we've missed

01:21:50.280 --> 01:21:52.020
and look for any red flags?

01:21:52.020 --> 01:21:53.130
Finally, rethink

01:21:53.130 --> 01:21:55.680
and that's where the advice
and guidance comes in.

01:21:55.680 --> 01:21:59.580
If appropriate, GP teams
will refer patients onwards

01:21:59.580 --> 01:22:02.340
for further tests and
that might mean a scan

01:22:02.340 --> 01:22:05.550
that they could request themselves
or a further assessment.

01:22:05.550 --> 01:22:07.290
And that would mean taking expert advice

01:22:07.290 --> 01:22:08.820
from a specialist colleague

01:22:08.820 --> 01:22:11.850
either through advice and
guidance or a referral.

01:22:11.850 --> 01:22:14.940
And where a referral is
converted back to advice

01:22:14.940 --> 01:22:17.310
and guidance by colleagues
in secondary care

01:22:17.310 --> 01:22:19.410
using that single point of access,

01:22:19.410 --> 01:22:20.970
the secondary care colleagues

01:22:20.970 --> 01:22:22.910
will directly communicate with the patient

01:22:22.910 --> 01:22:24.930
so there's absolute clarity.

01:22:24.930 --> 01:22:26.160
- Okay, thank you.

01:22:26.160 --> 01:22:30.930
So the second question is also
about advice and guidance.

01:22:30.930 --> 01:22:33.606
So recent amendments to paragraph 11C

01:22:33.606 --> 01:22:36.330
of the GMS regulations
require contractors to comply

01:22:36.330 --> 01:22:40.140
with notified referral pathways
defined to include advice

01:22:40.140 --> 01:22:43.740
and guidance only where it
is clinically appropriate.

01:22:43.740 --> 01:22:47.550
Can the board confirm whether
under any pathway notified

01:22:47.550 --> 01:22:51.120
or published by NHS
England, a GP who judges

01:22:51.120 --> 01:22:54.540
that direct referral is
clinically appropriate,

01:22:54.540 --> 01:22:56.700
would nonetheless be in contractual breach

01:22:56.700 --> 01:22:59.640
or required to formally
justify that judgement

01:22:59.640 --> 01:23:02.460
for not using advice and guidance first?

01:23:02.460 --> 01:23:04.350
And if so, how is that consistent

01:23:04.350 --> 01:23:05.966
with NHS England's published assurance

01:23:05.966 --> 01:23:08.958
of the 22nd of April, 2026,

01:23:08.958 --> 01:23:10.380
that the GP's clinical decision

01:23:10.380 --> 01:23:14.040
to refer remains unchanged in practise.

01:23:14.040 --> 01:23:15.233
- Thank you.

01:23:15.233 --> 01:23:18.300
So again, the short answer
is yes, the board can confirm

01:23:18.300 --> 01:23:20.659
that the contractual
requirement is qualified

01:23:20.659 --> 01:23:23.130
by clinical judgement.

01:23:23.130 --> 01:23:23.963
The regulations

01:23:23.963 --> 01:23:27.300
which will come into effect
mid-June, require contractors

01:23:27.300 --> 01:23:30.000
to comply with notified referral pathways,

01:23:30.000 --> 01:23:31.740
which include advice and guidance,

01:23:31.740 --> 01:23:34.350
but only where clinically appropriate.

01:23:34.350 --> 01:23:35.880
So where a GP determines

01:23:35.880 --> 01:23:38.580
that a direct referral is
clinically appropriate,

01:23:38.580 --> 01:23:40.620
that would not be a contractual breach.

01:23:40.620 --> 01:23:42.753
That is absolutely the right thing to do.

01:23:44.220 --> 01:23:46.770
There is no requirement for
them to go automatically

01:23:46.770 --> 01:23:49.110
through advice and guidance first.

01:23:49.110 --> 01:23:51.270
And this is consistent
with NHS England's letter

01:23:51.270 --> 01:23:53.940
of the 22nd of April, which is again clear

01:23:53.940 --> 01:23:57.627
that the clinical threshold
for referral is unchanged.

01:23:59.340 --> 01:24:02.049
And this is an additional choice,

01:24:02.049 --> 01:24:04.590
a different route, not instead.

01:24:04.590 --> 01:24:06.210
And the GP's clinical decision

01:24:06.210 --> 01:24:08.373
to refer is completely unchanged.

01:24:09.270 --> 01:24:12.870
There is no expectations that
GPs must provide a separate

01:24:12.870 --> 01:24:16.110
formal justification for
deciding whether it's a referral

01:24:16.110 --> 01:24:17.790
or advice and guidance.

01:24:17.790 --> 01:24:21.270
And where additional clinical
information is requested,

01:24:21.270 --> 01:24:24.990
that is through specific pathways.

01:24:24.990 --> 01:24:26.910
That is to support safe triage

01:24:26.910 --> 01:24:29.580
and safe decision making
under the advice given

01:24:29.580 --> 01:24:32.700
not to include a new contractual hurdle.

01:24:32.700 --> 01:24:34.200
So advice and guidance should be used

01:24:34.200 --> 01:24:35.610
where clinically appropriate

01:24:35.610 --> 01:24:37.140
and in line with notified pathways,

01:24:37.140 --> 01:24:40.920
but it is not intended to
cut across clinical judgement

01:24:40.920 --> 01:24:45.270
by GPs and is not a mandatory
prerequisite to referral.

01:24:45.270 --> 01:24:46.560
- Thank you.

01:24:46.560 --> 01:24:49.050
Another one on advice and guidance.

01:24:49.050 --> 01:24:52.740
So the Health Services
Safety Investigation Body

01:24:52.740 --> 01:24:56.040
is actively investigating
patient safety concerns

01:24:56.040 --> 01:24:58.740
arising from plans to mandate advice

01:24:58.740 --> 01:25:01.320
and guidance with an interim report

01:25:01.320 --> 01:25:03.720
due in August of this year.

01:25:03.720 --> 01:25:06.210
The Care Quality Commission
has reserved the right

01:25:06.210 --> 01:25:09.450
to trigger the emerging concerns protocol

01:25:09.450 --> 01:25:12.183
should risks remain unmitigated.

01:25:13.020 --> 01:25:14.220
Can the board explain

01:25:14.220 --> 01:25:17.010
how it could be appropriate
to mandate advice

01:25:17.010 --> 01:25:20.190
and guidance in any form
while an independent

01:25:20.190 --> 01:25:23.913
national patient safety
investigation remains outstanding?

01:25:24.780 --> 01:25:25.830
- So firstly, I'm sorry

01:25:25.830 --> 01:25:29.010
that there is still confusion
in this area, but advice

01:25:29.010 --> 01:25:33.270
and guidance is not and
has never been mandated.

01:25:33.270 --> 01:25:35.220
The health services
safety investigations body

01:25:35.220 --> 01:25:38.133
has also clarified that point,
which I hope is helpful.

01:25:39.360 --> 01:25:42.210
The current position is
that practises are expected

01:25:42.210 --> 01:25:45.210
to use advice and guidance prior to,

01:25:45.210 --> 01:25:49.260
or instead of a referral,
if clinically appropriate,

01:25:49.260 --> 01:25:51.930
alongside sending traditional referrals

01:25:51.930 --> 01:25:53.490
if that is clinically appropriate.

01:25:53.490 --> 01:25:56.553
So this is an and, not necessarily an all.

01:26:00.030 --> 01:26:01.620
Advice and guidance will support

01:26:01.620 --> 01:26:03.330
that clinical decision making.

01:26:03.330 --> 01:26:04.680
It does not override it

01:26:04.680 --> 01:26:07.440
and it does not remove the
clinician's responsibility

01:26:07.440 --> 01:26:10.353
to refer where that referral
is in the best interest.

01:26:11.880 --> 01:26:14.340
- And probably worth saying
that the purpose of advice

01:26:14.340 --> 01:26:16.950
and guidance is to facilitate rapid access

01:26:16.950 --> 01:26:19.380
specialist advice for
patients who need it.

01:26:19.380 --> 01:26:20.250
- Absolutely.

01:26:20.250 --> 01:26:23.340
And this has advice and
guidance is not new advice

01:26:23.340 --> 01:26:26.670
and guidance really is
a fundamental support

01:26:26.670 --> 01:26:28.830
of the relationship between
primary and secondary care.

01:26:28.830 --> 01:26:31.560
It's an easy way of getting advice quickly

01:26:31.560 --> 01:26:33.540
and so that you can improve the care

01:26:33.540 --> 01:26:35.370
for that patient as quickly as possible.

01:26:35.370 --> 01:26:36.720
- Okay, great. Thank you.

01:26:36.720 --> 01:26:38.670
Does anyone have any other business

01:26:38.670 --> 01:26:41.332
that they would like
to raise at this stage?

01:26:41.332 --> 01:26:42.300
No?

01:26:42.300 --> 01:26:43.650
Okay, thank you all very much.

01:26:43.650 --> 01:26:46.350
Thank you for some very
good and detailed materials

01:26:46.350 --> 01:26:48.780
and I look forward to the
next set of conversations.

01:26:48.780 --> 01:26:49.613
Thank you.

